WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.902 --> 00:00:15.079
- Good morning, everyone, I
have really nothing to say,

00:00:15.079 --> 00:00:18.187
other than, it's almost inexpressible

00:00:18.187 --> 00:00:20.687
how happy this event makes me,

00:00:21.983 --> 00:00:26.150
and to see so many familiar
faces gathered together

00:00:27.796 --> 00:00:31.629
to discuss Horace Walpole's
"Castle of Otranto,"

00:00:32.834 --> 00:00:36.465
and really, in so doing, to, to commit

00:00:36.465 --> 00:00:40.801
and bear witness to what
we care so deeply about,

00:00:40.801 --> 00:00:45.208
and our shared endeavor,
so I can't tell you

00:00:45.208 --> 00:00:47.343
how much I'm looking
forward to this afternoon.

00:00:47.343 --> 00:00:51.282
Welcome, and I'm now going
to pass the mic over.

00:00:51.282 --> 00:00:55.880
- It is a special occasion,
and I just want to,

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.412
since Annie's portrait is behind me,

00:00:58.412 --> 00:01:03.140
to honor the founders of
this wonderful resource

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:07.216
The Walpole Library,
Wilmarth and Annie Lewis,

00:01:07.216 --> 00:01:10.678
their vision, and their
scholarship really resounds

00:01:10.678 --> 00:01:15.317
to this moment, and this
treasure of a place is one

00:01:15.317 --> 00:01:19.317
that I've returned to
often, and with gratitude,

00:01:20.475 --> 00:01:22.475
and I welcome you to it.

00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:26.500
My job today is just to keep the time,

00:01:27.553 --> 00:01:31.272
basically, and we're going to be mindful

00:01:31.272 --> 00:01:34.697
of 10 minutes for each of the speakers.

00:01:34.697 --> 00:01:37.024
I do want to say, though,
by way of preface,

00:01:37.024 --> 00:01:41.278
this occasion reminds me
of a program we had here

00:01:41.278 --> 00:01:43.889
at the Walpole, which was a followup

00:01:43.889 --> 00:01:47.958
to Yale's Tercentennial
Conference on anti-theatricality,

00:01:47.958 --> 00:01:51.085
and it was called "The
Country House Weekend,"

00:01:51.085 --> 00:01:52.154
and those of us who were involved

00:01:52.154 --> 00:01:56.540
in the conference decided not
to do an edited collection,

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:58.688
but rather to meet, a year later,

00:01:58.688 --> 00:02:01.369
and talk about what we had
done at the conference,

00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:04.718
and what our work
generated since that point.

00:02:04.718 --> 00:02:08.230
And out of that came, not just
out of that conference alone,

00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:12.397
but gave its inflection to
Felicity Nussbaum's "Rival Queens,"

00:02:13.675 --> 00:02:16.503
to Lisa Freeman's book
on anti-theatricality,

00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:19.661
to Julie Stone Peters'
"The Theatre of the Book,"

00:02:19.661 --> 00:02:23.828
to my work "It" and others that
came out of this opportunity.

00:02:25.232 --> 00:02:27.321
So I want to capture some of the spirit,

00:02:27.321 --> 00:02:28.733
although I don't know that we'll have 10

00:02:28.733 --> 00:02:30.590
or 12 books on "Castle of Otranto."

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:31.537
(group laughs)

00:02:31.537 --> 00:02:36.028
Excellent as that would be,
I hope it will be generative

00:02:36.028 --> 00:02:38.424
in that way, because it
was the conversation,

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:41.766
it was the sense of the
galvanic intellectual moment

00:02:41.766 --> 00:02:44.131
of we, who love the 18th century,

00:02:44.131 --> 00:02:47.489
gathered together to
talk about one of its,

00:02:47.489 --> 00:02:49.276
one of its works, I hope it will generate

00:02:49.276 --> 00:02:51.708
that, the kind of excitement it did

00:02:51.708 --> 00:02:53.249
on that previous occasion,
as a matter of fact,

00:02:53.249 --> 00:02:56.322
I'm confident it will,
knowing these speakers.

00:02:56.322 --> 00:02:59.471
I'm going to introduce each
of the speakers in order,

00:02:59.471 --> 00:03:02.327
and then let them take the floor.

00:03:02.327 --> 00:03:04.402
First, let me welcome Sandra Macpherson,

00:03:04.402 --> 00:03:07.696
Associate Professor of English
at Ohio State University,

00:03:07.696 --> 00:03:09.211
author, of course, of "Harm's Way:

00:03:09.211 --> 00:03:12.221
"Tragic Responsibility and the Novel Form,"

00:03:12.221 --> 00:03:16.388
from Hopkins in 2010, and an
essay of the political fallacy,

00:03:17.512 --> 00:03:21.515
which is due out in "PMLA"
next month, I believe, so.

00:03:21.515 --> 00:03:24.456
This month, yeah, right,
okay, it's now alert.

00:03:24.456 --> 00:03:26.146
(group laughs)

00:03:26.146 --> 00:03:29.700
"PMLA" right now, and coming, a book called

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:33.525
"The Shape of Form," next, Jesse Molesworth,

00:03:33.525 --> 00:03:36.387
on my left, Associate Professor of English

00:03:36.387 --> 00:03:39.153
at Indiana University, and,
of course, you will know him

00:03:39.153 --> 00:03:42.278
from "Chance and the
Eighteenth-Century Novel:

00:03:42.278 --> 00:03:44.945
"Realism, Probability, and Magic."

00:03:48.113 --> 00:03:49.685
At the end of the table there on my left,

00:03:49.685 --> 00:03:52.629
Sean Silver is Associate
Professor of Literature

00:03:52.629 --> 00:03:55.239
at the University of Michigan,
he is currently working

00:03:55.239 --> 00:03:59.317
on a history of complexity,
from its conceptual origins

00:03:59.317 --> 00:04:01.505
in the philosophy, crafts, and arts

00:04:01.505 --> 00:04:04.518
of 17th century Britain to its emergence

00:04:04.518 --> 00:04:08.685
in theories of self-organization
in the mid 19th century.

00:04:09.644 --> 00:04:11.759
That's a lot in a
sentence, but I just want

00:04:11.759 --> 00:04:15.541
to give a moment of a shout out to a book

00:04:15.541 --> 00:04:18.904
that I've been editing with Maggie,

00:04:18.904 --> 00:04:22.674
former director here at The Lewis Walpole,

00:04:22.674 --> 00:04:26.040
Maggie Powell, on hair
in the 18th century,

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:29.957
and the essay, by Sean,
is related to this work

00:04:31.145 --> 00:04:34.562
about hair as product, as master metaphor

00:04:35.783 --> 00:04:40.034
for the underlying organization
of intellectual life,

00:04:40.034 --> 00:04:42.478
and practical life in the 18th century,

00:04:42.478 --> 00:04:45.540
so I'm, I just want to make that note

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.285
about the connection there.

00:04:48.285 --> 00:04:51.421
Then, of course, Cynthia
Wall, Professor of English

00:04:51.421 --> 00:04:53.814
at The University of Virginia, author

00:04:53.814 --> 00:04:56.572
of "The Prose of Things: Transformations

00:04:56.572 --> 00:04:58.889
"of Description in the Eighteenth Century,"

00:04:58.889 --> 00:05:02.316
and "The Literary and
Cultural Spaces of Restoration."

00:05:02.316 --> 00:05:04.819
And here, I just want to make a shout out

00:05:04.819 --> 00:05:07.106
to the work of Professor Wall,

00:05:07.106 --> 00:05:09.066
which has been generative in my own,

00:05:09.066 --> 00:05:10.727
I just want to say that publicly,

00:05:10.727 --> 00:05:14.029
and put it on the record as
we here, for posterity.

00:05:14.029 --> 00:05:16.401
(group laughs)

00:05:16.401 --> 00:05:17.234
And then,

00:05:19.381 --> 00:05:22.903
Eugenia Zuroski is Associate
Professor of English

00:05:22.903 --> 00:05:25.931
and Cultural Studies
at McMaster University,

00:05:25.931 --> 00:05:29.174
and editor of the journal
"Eighteenth-Century Fiction,"

00:05:29.174 --> 00:05:31.588
she's writing a book about funniness,

00:05:31.588 --> 00:05:35.234
and has a lot to say about
Walpole's nautilus cup,

00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:37.826
in case you're interested,
and of course we are.

00:05:37.826 --> 00:05:38.990
(group laughs)

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:41.882
We insist on your account of that,

00:05:41.882 --> 00:05:45.898
as well as your account
of "The Castle of Otranto."

00:05:45.898 --> 00:05:50.090
So that is my introduction,
and without further ado,

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:52.673
I turn it over to our speakers.

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:55.425
(overlapping chatter)

00:05:55.425 --> 00:05:57.103
- It's me, right?
- It is.

00:05:57.103 --> 00:05:59.294
- Okay, thank you so much, Joe,

00:05:59.294 --> 00:06:01.825
and thank you to Jonathan, and to Nicole,

00:06:01.825 --> 00:06:05.506
and everyone involved in arranging
this extraordinary event,

00:06:05.506 --> 00:06:09.767
I'm so moved and honored to
be here among people I respect

00:06:09.767 --> 00:06:12.586
so much, many of whose books

00:06:12.586 --> 00:06:15.696
and essays I've been
teaching this semester,

00:06:15.696 --> 00:06:19.655
so it's an especial treat
to see you in the flesh.

00:06:19.655 --> 00:06:22.240
So I'm a bit chagrined to be going first,

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:24.738
since I'm going to be violating the law

00:06:24.738 --> 00:06:27.286
that Jonathan set down right off the bat,

00:06:27.286 --> 00:06:29.856
which was to be, you know, spontaneous,

00:06:29.856 --> 00:06:31.835
which I'm incapable of being.

00:06:31.835 --> 00:06:32.918
(group laughs)

00:06:32.918 --> 00:06:36.552
So, because I can't think
without writing some stuff,

00:06:36.552 --> 00:06:40.689
so here's my recent thoughts
on "The Castle of Otranto."

00:06:40.689 --> 00:06:43.367
So I return to "The Castle
of Otranto" in the context

00:06:43.367 --> 00:06:45.867
of a graduate seminar I'm
teaching this semester

00:06:45.867 --> 00:06:48.829
on description, we began
with theoretical work

00:06:48.829 --> 00:06:51.014
in literary criticism and art history

00:06:51.014 --> 00:06:53.773
on the descriptive turn, post critique,

00:06:53.773 --> 00:06:56.404
surface reading, ekphrasis, still life,

00:06:56.404 --> 00:06:59.089
formalism, and we've been
moving, week by week,

00:06:59.089 --> 00:07:01.988
through descriptive genres
in modes of writing,

00:07:01.988 --> 00:07:06.068
from Cowper's "Tale" to "120 Days of Sodom."

00:07:06.068 --> 00:07:09.146
So the question motivating
this endeavor, at least for me,

00:07:09.146 --> 00:07:12.487
is what would a descriptive
poetics look like,

00:07:12.487 --> 00:07:15.376
not only for our field, but in general.

00:07:15.376 --> 00:07:18.811
Would it be a kind of ekphrasis
itself, is it possible

00:07:18.811 --> 00:07:22.774
to describe verbal objects
without interpreting them,

00:07:22.774 --> 00:07:26.391
can or does literary
criticism practice literal

00:07:26.391 --> 00:07:28.780
or surface reading?

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:32.063
It seems to me that, for
the most part, we don't.

00:07:32.063 --> 00:07:34.909
Heather Love, for example,
seeks to appropriate,

00:07:34.909 --> 00:07:37.528
for our discipline,
forms of close attention

00:07:37.528 --> 00:07:40.217
that, because they offer descriptions

00:07:40.217 --> 00:07:43.350
of surfaces, operations, and interactions,

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:47.231
bypass, she says, the
metaphysical and humanist concerns

00:07:47.231 --> 00:07:49.868
of hermeneutics, but when she turns

00:07:49.868 --> 00:07:53.728
to describe a literary text,
Toni Morrison's "Beloved,"

00:07:53.728 --> 00:07:56.670
description looks a lot
like interpretation.

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:59.682
Love says she wants to
follow up on a suggestion

00:07:59.682 --> 00:08:03.386
by Stanley Crouch, mostly
ignored by later critics,

00:08:03.386 --> 00:08:05.401
that Morrison's primary gift is one

00:08:05.401 --> 00:08:09.378
of neutral detailed description; quote,

00:08:09.378 --> 00:08:13.220
"He cites a passage approvingly
where Sethe makes biscuits,"

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:16.060
she recounts, yet she also insists

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:18.184
that biscuit making is not,

00:08:18.184 --> 00:08:20.428
and can never be mere biscuit making,

00:08:20.428 --> 00:08:22.583
that instead of providing a background

00:08:22.583 --> 00:08:25.687
for the events of the novel,
Morrison's descriptions are

00:08:25.687 --> 00:08:29.854
a vehicle for a critique
of historical reclamation.

00:08:31.385 --> 00:08:34.595
Indeed, she concludes
that Morrison's account

00:08:34.595 --> 00:08:36.662
of dehumanization contrasts

00:08:36.662 --> 00:08:39.914
with Goffman's assiduously
neutral portraits

00:08:39.914 --> 00:08:43.824
of social interactions,
and with Latour's refusal

00:08:43.824 --> 00:08:45.717
of humanist ethics.

00:08:45.717 --> 00:08:49.970
And she remains quite
committed to this contrast,

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:53.715
"A turn from interpretation
to description might be a way

00:08:53.715 --> 00:08:56.949
"of giving up the ghost of
a hermeneutics of empathy

00:08:56.949 --> 00:08:59.330
"and recognition," she reflects,

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:02.999
"but who among us is
willing to exchange the fat

00:09:02.999 --> 00:09:06.941
"and the living for
the thin and the dead?"

00:09:06.941 --> 00:09:11.127
So I think maybe I'm willing
to make that exchange.

00:09:11.127 --> 00:09:12.032
(group laughs)

00:09:12.032 --> 00:09:14.592
And I think maybe Walpole is willing,

00:09:14.592 --> 00:09:17.868
and perhaps the gothic
is entirely predicated

00:09:17.868 --> 00:09:20.699
on this exchange of the fat and the living

00:09:20.699 --> 00:09:22.576
for the thin and the dead.

00:09:22.576 --> 00:09:25.391
But even this is too substantive a claim

00:09:25.391 --> 00:09:28.238
for a rigorously descriptive poetics.

00:09:28.238 --> 00:09:29.718
As I said, I'm not sure

00:09:29.718 --> 00:09:33.150
what Goffman's assiduous
neutrality would look like

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:35.506
for literary criticism, maybe I will know

00:09:35.506 --> 00:09:37.298
by the end of the semester.

00:09:37.298 --> 00:09:39.882
For now, I'm simply going to
share with you my experience

00:09:39.882 --> 00:09:41.901
of returning to "The Castle of Otranto"

00:09:41.901 --> 00:09:43.957
with a different set of questions

00:09:43.957 --> 00:09:47.759
and commitments than I had the
last time I read the novel.

00:09:47.759 --> 00:09:50.462
Then, I was reading it
for my book, "Harm's Way,"

00:09:50.462 --> 00:09:53.701
and I was tracking the logic
of action at a distance

00:09:53.701 --> 00:09:56.769
of proximate causation,
of strict liability,

00:09:56.769 --> 00:10:00.409
I was interested in how Jerome
and Hippolita are causes

00:10:00.409 --> 00:10:02.724
of Matilda's murder, the former

00:10:02.724 --> 00:10:04.905
because he creates the sexual jealousy

00:10:04.905 --> 00:10:08.047
that sends Manfred after
Theodore and Isabella,

00:10:08.047 --> 00:10:11.231
the latter because she
literally dispatches Matilda

00:10:11.231 --> 00:10:15.471
to Theodore as an intercessor
on behalf of Manfred.

00:10:15.471 --> 00:10:17.795
Had I managed to get my
thoughts together enough

00:10:17.795 --> 00:10:20.538
to include "The Castle
of Otranto" in my book,

00:10:20.538 --> 00:10:22.266
I might have said something like,

00:10:22.266 --> 00:10:24.545
"The Catholic logic of intercession,

00:10:24.545 --> 00:10:27.992
"the practice of deputizing
an agent to act on one's own

00:10:27.992 --> 00:10:30.579
"or a third party's
behalf is more essential

00:10:30.579 --> 00:10:33.069
"to the way the novel
works through the problem

00:10:33.069 --> 00:10:34.900
"of responsibility than the idea

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:39.350
"of original sin pointed
to in Walpole's preface."

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:41.778
I would have attended to the centrality

00:10:41.778 --> 00:10:44.328
of object agency, thing liability,

00:10:44.328 --> 00:10:47.043
and its converse, the instrumentalization,

00:10:47.043 --> 00:10:49.447
de-personification of human agents,

00:10:49.447 --> 00:10:51.895
and I would have insisted
that strict liability

00:10:51.895 --> 00:10:53.451
for the effects of actions,

00:10:53.451 --> 00:10:58.102
not in any immediate way
one's own, is the gothic.

00:10:58.102 --> 00:10:59.496
I might still say this,

00:10:59.496 --> 00:11:02.887
and I guess I actually have just said it,

00:11:02.887 --> 00:11:05.662
but in the book I'm completing now,

00:11:05.662 --> 00:11:08.864
these are the sorts of
things that interest me.

00:11:08.864 --> 00:11:10.938
Although in the preface
to the second edition,

00:11:10.938 --> 00:11:14.083
Walpole satirizes a naive mimesis

00:11:14.083 --> 00:11:17.636
that would have Rosencrantz
and Guildenstern describe,

00:11:17.636 --> 00:11:21.230
or Rosencrantz describe for
Guildenstern the iconography

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:24.926
of the palace of Copenhagen,
"The Castle of Otranto,"

00:11:24.926 --> 00:11:28.066
at least, for this reader,
is all about doors,

00:11:28.066 --> 00:11:33.033
gates, windows, floors,
chambers, casements, and stairs.

00:11:33.033 --> 00:11:35.083
The dominant formal
feature of the novel is

00:11:35.083 --> 00:11:38.328
what one might call
chronotropic description,

00:11:38.328 --> 00:11:41.427
a tropology of space and movement.

00:11:41.427 --> 00:11:43.986
This is not, I think,
equivalent to what Cindy,

00:11:43.986 --> 00:11:47.312
via Henry Peacham, calls pragmatographia,

00:11:47.312 --> 00:11:50.295
here is no investment in energia,

00:11:50.295 --> 00:11:52.876
in vividness of description, rather,

00:11:52.876 --> 00:11:54.329
these architectural forms are

00:11:54.329 --> 00:11:57.659
what George Kugler would
call "primary objects,"

00:11:57.659 --> 00:12:00.864
forms whose functions is
to be undifferentiated,

00:12:00.864 --> 00:12:04.371
the same shape always and everywhere.

00:12:04.371 --> 00:12:06.031
The importance of these forms,

00:12:06.031 --> 00:12:08.243
the way they take up
space, get in the way,

00:12:08.243 --> 00:12:10.471
especially of the
unfolding of the incidents

00:12:10.471 --> 00:12:13.897
of plot and their meaning is parodied

00:12:13.897 --> 00:12:17.671
in the slapstick exchange
between Jaquez and Diego,

00:12:17.671 --> 00:12:19.955
who frustrate Manfred's desire to know

00:12:19.955 --> 00:12:22.711
what they have seen by
discoursing at large

00:12:22.711 --> 00:12:25.462
on how doors open and
shut, and it's that great,

00:12:25.462 --> 00:12:27.379
you know, clown moment.

00:12:28.458 --> 00:12:31.314
Later, this parody is
ratcheted up in the procession

00:12:31.314 --> 00:12:33.613
of the stranger knight and his train,

00:12:33.613 --> 00:12:37.285
which is a cavalcade of
nothing so much as metonymy,

00:12:37.285 --> 00:12:40.524
two trumpets, 100 foot
guards, as many horse,

00:12:40.524 --> 00:12:42.794
each phrase an example of the indexing

00:12:42.794 --> 00:12:45.148
of an object, in this
case, a human person,

00:12:45.148 --> 00:12:46.648
by her attributes.

00:12:47.733 --> 00:12:51.276
Manfred, for his part, is a
parvenu of suspicious reading,

00:12:51.276 --> 00:12:54.415
coming upon Conrad's body
mangled beneath the helmet,

00:12:54.415 --> 00:12:57.048
he's entirely indifferent
to the banal facticity

00:12:57.048 --> 00:12:59.082
of the corpse, preferring, instead,

00:12:59.082 --> 00:13:02.190
to focus attention on
the object that calls out

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:07.095
to be read allegorically, or
emblematically, the helmet.

00:13:07.095 --> 00:13:08.977
Hippolita, on the other
hand, is indifferent

00:13:08.977 --> 00:13:12.153
to anything but the death
of her son, we're told.

00:13:12.153 --> 00:13:15.268
So at the very outset,
Walpole stages an opposition

00:13:15.268 --> 00:13:17.786
between literal and hermeneutic attention,

00:13:17.786 --> 00:13:21.297
and indeed, one could say
that the novel is a fantasia

00:13:21.297 --> 00:13:23.819
of literalization, and in particular,

00:13:23.819 --> 00:13:26.456
a literalizing of verbal artifacts,

00:13:26.456 --> 00:13:29.496
a kind of reverse ekphrasis of the clause,

00:13:29.496 --> 00:13:32.132
grown too large to inhabit.

00:13:32.132 --> 00:13:34.156
This is one way to
understand the other thing

00:13:34.156 --> 00:13:37.379
that is currently obsessing
me, which is the sheer number

00:13:37.379 --> 00:13:39.806
of exclamation points and em dashes

00:13:39.806 --> 00:13:42.770
in the orthography and
typography of the text.

00:13:42.770 --> 00:13:45.399
This, too, is literalization, we're told

00:13:45.399 --> 00:13:47.803
that Manfred is brought to his senses

00:13:47.803 --> 00:13:52.147
to register Conrad's demise
by a folly of ejaculations,

00:13:52.147 --> 00:13:54.303
and what is "The Castle of Otranto"

00:13:54.303 --> 00:13:57.189
but such a folly of ejaculation?

00:13:57.189 --> 00:14:00.046
The novel is built upon
intensifying scenes

00:14:00.046 --> 00:14:02.897
of exclamatory and interrogative speech,

00:14:02.897 --> 00:14:04.370
and the two practices

00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:07.987
of literalization are brought together,

00:14:07.987 --> 00:14:11.237
literalizing in objects, lexical units,

00:14:13.077 --> 00:14:17.676
and literalizing the content
of the, the affective content

00:14:17.676 --> 00:14:21.394
of lexical units in punctuation
are brought together

00:14:21.394 --> 00:14:23.732
as the novel unfolds,
especially in the scene

00:14:23.732 --> 00:14:25.914
between Manfred and the sighing knight,

00:14:25.914 --> 00:14:30.061
where the former's ejaculations
register speech on the part

00:14:30.061 --> 00:14:33.262
of the knight that, as a
narratologist might say,

00:14:33.262 --> 00:14:37.132
is disnarrated, pointed
to, but not represented,

00:14:37.132 --> 00:14:40.777
a deixis, an act of pointing
that points to, instead,

00:14:40.777 --> 00:14:43.925
bodily morphology, the shake of the head

00:14:43.925 --> 00:14:46.779
that is the identical
characteristic movement

00:14:46.779 --> 00:14:49.077
of the helmet's plumes.

00:14:49.077 --> 00:14:50.970
So this is about as far as I've managed

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:53.935
to get in my thinking,
someone else might want

00:14:53.935 --> 00:14:57.204
to say something substantive
about fragmentation,

00:14:57.204 --> 00:15:00.261
interruption, the link
between these formal features,

00:15:00.261 --> 00:15:03.793
and the thematics of
usurpation, but in conclusion,

00:15:03.793 --> 00:15:06.386
I just want to point
to Bianca's description

00:15:06.386 --> 00:15:09.780
of the gigantic, animated statue.

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:13.030
"It," she says, "as big, as big, dash."

00:15:15.097 --> 00:15:18.833
A failed simile that, in
eschewing indexicality,

00:15:18.833 --> 00:15:21.514
prevents reading, a deixis that points

00:15:21.514 --> 00:15:24.991
to the attributes of a
thing, without explaining it.

00:15:24.991 --> 00:15:26.683
This, in a very literal sense,

00:15:26.683 --> 00:15:29.292
is what "The Castle of Otranto," for me,

00:15:29.292 --> 00:15:32.875
is about, if "aboutness"
is the right word.

00:15:33.958 --> 00:15:36.708
(group applauds)

00:15:41.767 --> 00:15:42.600
- Jesse.

00:15:44.559 --> 00:15:45.848
- Thanks to everyone, this is a,

00:15:45.848 --> 00:15:50.018
you know, been a fabulous
event, and thank you for coming.

00:15:50.018 --> 00:15:52.226
So this paper is an extension
of an article I wrote

00:15:52.226 --> 00:15:54.097
on Walpole several years ago,

00:15:54.097 --> 00:15:57.027
called "Gothic Time, Sacred Time,"

00:15:57.027 --> 00:15:59.733
and in that essay, I argued
that there is such a thing

00:15:59.733 --> 00:16:03.259
as the gothic clock trope, that is to say

00:16:03.259 --> 00:16:06.457
that in the gothic time is
not really out of joint,

00:16:06.457 --> 00:16:08.886
as someone like Derrida argues, rather,

00:16:08.886 --> 00:16:12.225
it's both out of joint,
but also aggressively

00:16:12.225 --> 00:16:14.112
what I call "in joint," that is to say

00:16:14.112 --> 00:16:17.293
that there's a precision to
the timing of the actions

00:16:17.293 --> 00:16:21.533
of events in the gothic,
and especially an interest

00:16:21.533 --> 00:16:24.295
in the hour of their occurrence,

00:16:24.295 --> 00:16:26.275
and maybe the emblem for that
might be the bleeding nun

00:16:26.275 --> 00:16:29.951
in "The Monk," who appears
on the 5th of May,

00:16:29.951 --> 00:16:34.118
every fifth year, as soon
as the clock strikes 1:00.

00:16:35.236 --> 00:16:37.169
So the point that I made in this essay was

00:16:37.169 --> 00:16:39.819
that this phenomenon seems rooted

00:16:39.819 --> 00:16:43.289
in a very odd decision to me,
and that is Walpole's decision

00:16:43.289 --> 00:16:46.616
to write a novel that
obeys the unity of time.

00:16:46.616 --> 00:16:48.200
That is to say that, you know,

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:50.682
we know that this plot unfolds over a,

00:16:50.682 --> 00:16:54.500
you know, roughly, evenly
spaced period of three days,

00:16:54.500 --> 00:16:57.204
it's in the same location,
so it also obeys the unity

00:16:57.204 --> 00:17:00.021
of space, during these three days,

00:17:00.021 --> 00:17:02.924
the passage of time is regularly marked,

00:17:02.924 --> 00:17:05.210
not really by the number of the hour,

00:17:05.210 --> 00:17:08.506
but by natural signifiers,
sunrise, sunset,

00:17:08.506 --> 00:17:11.851
but also by supernatural
signifiers, as well,

00:17:11.851 --> 00:17:14.380
so that, you know, the
appearance of the helmet,

00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:18.346
the foot, the sword,
the arm of Alfonso seem

00:17:18.346 --> 00:17:20.685
to operate like ticks in some type

00:17:20.685 --> 00:17:23.602
of clock that is gradually winding.

00:17:24.928 --> 00:17:28.171
In any case, there's a, you
know, a clock-like treatment

00:17:28.171 --> 00:17:30.493
of time in "The Castle of Otranto."

00:17:30.493 --> 00:17:32.754
I mean, it's always been shocking,

00:17:32.754 --> 00:17:35.824
shockingly odd to me, the
interest in the unity of time,

00:17:35.824 --> 00:17:39.728
and you might say, well, it
creates the dramatic structure

00:17:39.728 --> 00:17:42.641
of the novel, in the sense
that the five chapters feel

00:17:42.641 --> 00:17:44.769
like the five acts of a play, and,

00:17:44.769 --> 00:17:47.916
you know, maybe it enhances
the Shakespearean character

00:17:47.916 --> 00:17:50.284
of the novel, but we
also have to remember,

00:17:50.284 --> 00:17:51.913
of course, that Shakespeare is, you know,

00:17:51.913 --> 00:17:54.806
famous ultimately for denying the unities,

00:17:54.806 --> 00:17:56.284
and so there's something, you know,

00:17:56.284 --> 00:17:58.561
I think also profoundly un-Shakespearean

00:17:58.561 --> 00:18:01.004
about that decision.

00:18:01.004 --> 00:18:03.886
So, in any case, since I wrote that essay,

00:18:03.886 --> 00:18:06.585
which was on Walpole,
and Lewis, and Radcliffe,

00:18:06.585 --> 00:18:07.506
I've become interested in kind

00:18:07.506 --> 00:18:11.421
of tracking this gothic clock
trope into subsequent fictions

00:18:11.421 --> 00:18:12.966
that have been influenced by the gothic,

00:18:12.966 --> 00:18:15.969
so "Wuthering Heights," "Great Expectations,"

00:18:15.969 --> 00:18:18.572
and the rest of this paper's
actually on "Mrs. Dalloway."

00:18:18.572 --> 00:18:19.405
(group laughs)

00:18:19.405 --> 00:18:21.817
Which is a novel that,
you know, I think is,

00:18:21.817 --> 00:18:24.743
you know, increasingly seen
within the gothic tradition,

00:18:24.743 --> 00:18:28.426
particularly following
Paul Saint Amour's book.

00:18:28.426 --> 00:18:30.703
Virginia Woolf's novel, "Mrs.
Dalloway," features an array

00:18:30.703 --> 00:18:33.157
of clocks, meticulously
marking the passage

00:18:33.157 --> 00:18:38.013
of time over the course of
a single June day, 1923.

00:18:38.013 --> 00:18:39.209
The Westminster neighborhood in

00:18:39.209 --> 00:18:40.872
which the novel's primarily set,

00:18:40.872 --> 00:18:44.264
two clocks joust with
one another for primacy.

00:18:44.264 --> 00:18:46.000
There is, of course, the masculine figure

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:47.754
of Big Ben, whose ringing is heard

00:18:47.754 --> 00:18:51.555
on five separate occasions,
and there is St. Margaret's,

00:18:51.555 --> 00:18:53.870
the church whose bells are personified

00:18:53.870 --> 00:18:56.385
as the feminine counterpart to Big Ben,

00:18:56.385 --> 00:18:57.530
so this is from the novel.

00:18:57.530 --> 00:19:00.739
"Ah, said St. Margaret's,
like the hostess who comes

00:19:00.739 --> 00:19:04.536
"into her drawing room on
the very stroke of the hour,

00:19:04.536 --> 00:19:09.309
"and finds her guests there
already, I am not late."

00:19:09.309 --> 00:19:10.882
Big Ben and St. Margaret's, therefore,

00:19:10.882 --> 00:19:13.452
express themselves as versions of Richard

00:19:13.452 --> 00:19:14.564
and Clarissa Dalloway,

00:19:14.564 --> 00:19:17.364
the married couple whose story
forms the core of the novel.

00:19:17.364 --> 00:19:19.922
Set to ring two minutes after Big Ben,

00:19:19.922 --> 00:19:22.410
St. Margaret's seems
scarcely more than an echo

00:19:22.410 --> 00:19:24.724
of its more prominent competitor,

00:19:24.724 --> 00:19:26.908
much in the way that
Clarissa now devotes much

00:19:26.908 --> 00:19:29.526
of her own life to supporting the work

00:19:29.526 --> 00:19:34.331
and professional life of her
husband, a conservative MP.

00:19:34.331 --> 00:19:37.415
The setup is therefore not
unlike that found in "The Monk,"

00:19:37.415 --> 00:19:40.176
in which the masculine monastery bell

00:19:40.176 --> 00:19:42.400
and the feminine convent bell joust

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:44.549
with one another, sending the students,

00:19:44.549 --> 00:19:47.049
or the citizens of Lewis' Madrid

00:19:47.049 --> 00:19:50.571
to perform tasks at their appointed hour.

00:19:50.571 --> 00:19:53.013
"Mrs. Dalloway," it is Big Ben, for example,

00:19:53.013 --> 00:19:55.741
that informs us that the
time is the early morning,

00:19:55.741 --> 00:19:59.142
during the novel's opening
scene, 10 o'clock, seemingly,

00:19:59.142 --> 00:20:01.022
when Clarissa sets off to buy flowers

00:20:01.022 --> 00:20:03.906
for the party she is
scheduled to host later.

00:20:03.906 --> 00:20:07.974
"So there, out it boomed,
first a warning musical,

00:20:07.974 --> 00:20:11.748
"than the hour, irrevocable,
the leaden circles dissolved

00:20:11.748 --> 00:20:16.138
"in the air," in its four
subsequent appearances,

00:20:16.138 --> 00:20:20.270
Big Ben marks the time very
carefully as 11:30 a.m.,

00:20:20.270 --> 00:20:22.770
noon, 3:00 p.m., and 3:30 p.m.

00:20:23.899 --> 00:20:26.967
The effect, therefore, is
partially to instill a sense

00:20:26.967 --> 00:20:29.671
of time discipline on a novel that lurches

00:20:29.671 --> 00:20:33.310
through various memories
across vast stretches of time,

00:20:33.310 --> 00:20:36.270
more insistently, though,
Big Ben's carefully

00:20:36.270 --> 00:20:39.430
marked ringings bear
out the formal oddness

00:20:39.430 --> 00:20:42.896
that the novel happens to share
with "The Castle of Otranto,"

00:20:42.896 --> 00:20:46.465
it also obeys Aristotle's
unity of time, and in fact,

00:20:46.465 --> 00:20:47.514
you might say that this is one

00:20:47.514 --> 00:20:50.903
of the very few novels written
since "The Castle of Otranto"

00:20:50.903 --> 00:20:53.882
that obeys the unity of time.

00:20:53.882 --> 00:20:57.194
"Mrs. Dalloway," of course,
features no actual ghosts,

00:20:57.194 --> 00:20:59.827
but it does feature an array
of characters who are haunted

00:20:59.827 --> 00:21:02.877
in various ways, according
to the logic suggested

00:21:02.877 --> 00:21:06.283
by Terry Castle, where the
mind has become a world

00:21:06.283 --> 00:21:10.514
of phantoms, consider the
case of Septimus Warren Smith,

00:21:10.514 --> 00:21:12.247
the poet and soldier whose story

00:21:12.247 --> 00:21:15.675
of shellshocked trauma
intersects with the main plot.

00:21:15.675 --> 00:21:18.335
Between these two roles, poet and soldier,

00:21:18.335 --> 00:21:20.585
critics have had much
to say about the latter,

00:21:20.585 --> 00:21:23.043
and little to say about the former.

00:21:23.043 --> 00:21:26.285
This imbalance is unfortunate,
first because the poet's love

00:21:26.285 --> 00:21:28.845
of words expresses itself strongly

00:21:28.845 --> 00:21:31.735
throughout Septimus' internal monologues,

00:21:31.735 --> 00:21:35.359
and second, because
words frequently trigger

00:21:35.359 --> 00:21:39.484
Septimus' traumatic flashbacks
to his life as a soldier,

00:21:39.484 --> 00:21:43.499
and one of these words happens to be time.

00:21:43.499 --> 00:21:47.569
So this is from the novel,
"'It is time,' said Rezia,

00:21:47.569 --> 00:21:51.674
"the word time split its husk,
poured its riches over him,

00:21:51.674 --> 00:21:53.693
"and from his lips fell like shells,

00:21:53.693 --> 00:21:57.110
"like shavings from a plane,
without making them hard,

00:21:57.110 --> 00:22:00.194
"white, imperishable words, and flew

00:22:00.194 --> 00:22:02.583
"to attach themselves to their place

00:22:02.583 --> 00:22:06.721
"in an ode to time, an
immortal ode to time.

00:22:06.721 --> 00:22:09.745
"He sang, Evans sang among the orchids,

00:22:09.745 --> 00:22:12.507
"there they waited for the
war, til the war was over,

00:22:12.507 --> 00:22:14.961
"and now the dead, now Evans himself.

00:22:14.961 --> 00:22:17.755
"'For God's sake, don't
come,' Septimus cried out,

00:22:17.755 --> 00:22:20.868
"for he could not look upon the dead."

00:22:20.868 --> 00:22:24.068
Under his command as poet,
the word "time" was once

00:22:24.068 --> 00:22:27.463
an ally attached to his
most ambitious poem,

00:22:27.463 --> 00:22:29.767
"An Immortal Ode to Time," here, though,

00:22:29.767 --> 00:22:32.752
it has become his enemy,
attached to both the memory

00:22:32.752 --> 00:22:36.197
of his dead friend, Evans,
whose spirit haunts Septimus

00:22:36.197 --> 00:22:39.770
throughout the novel, and
to the unwelcome suggestion

00:22:39.770 --> 00:22:41.951
from his wife, that his psychiatrist,

00:22:41.951 --> 00:22:45.034
Dr. Bradshaw, is expecting them soon.

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:47.867
In fact, in the hands of Dr. Bradshaw,

00:22:47.867 --> 00:22:50.623
time has become something else entirely.

00:22:50.623 --> 00:22:54.376
Whereas for the poet, Septimus,
it is an abundant resource,

00:22:54.376 --> 00:22:57.222
something that pours its riches over him,

00:22:57.222 --> 00:23:00.998
for the psychiatrist, Bradshaw,
it is a scarce commodity,

00:23:00.998 --> 00:23:03.559
something to be managed and rationed.

00:23:03.559 --> 00:23:07.047
To his patients, he gave
three quarters of an hour.

00:23:07.047 --> 00:23:10.206
Woolf's personification of
Big Ben as a masculine source

00:23:10.206 --> 00:23:12.927
of authority, therefore reaches its zenith

00:23:12.927 --> 00:23:16.048
in its presentation of
Bradshaw, the psychiatrist.

00:23:16.048 --> 00:23:19.042
In the mind of someone
who worships proportion,

00:23:19.042 --> 00:23:21.234
divine proportion, Big Ben might be seen

00:23:21.234 --> 00:23:24.771
as its earthly minister,
carving hours proportionally

00:23:24.771 --> 00:23:27.423
into quarters, and coaching its subjects

00:23:27.423 --> 00:23:30.774
on the practice of
efficient time management.

00:23:30.774 --> 00:23:32.056
Eventually, of course,

00:23:32.056 --> 00:23:35.568
Septimus' dementia will
transform Bradshaw,

00:23:35.568 --> 00:23:38.408
along with Holmes, the
physician, into minions

00:23:38.408 --> 00:23:42.237
of a Lucifarian brute with red nostrils,

00:23:42.237 --> 00:23:43.963
but the novel itself has already hinted

00:23:43.963 --> 00:23:47.468
at Bradshaw's status as gothic tormentor,

00:23:47.468 --> 00:23:49.909
so this is from the novel, last passage,

00:23:49.909 --> 00:23:52.999
"It was precisely 12
o'clock, 12:00 by Big Ben,

00:23:52.999 --> 00:23:55.258
"whose stroke was wafted
over the norther part

00:23:55.258 --> 00:23:57.769
"of London, blent with
that of the other clocks,

00:23:57.769 --> 00:23:59.867
"mixed with a thin, ethereal way,

00:23:59.867 --> 00:24:02.149
"with the clouds and wisps of smokes,

00:24:02.149 --> 00:24:04.836
"and died up there among the seagulls,

00:24:04.836 --> 00:24:07.038
"12:00 was the hour of their appointment."

00:24:07.038 --> 00:24:10.000
So in the chapter that I
focus on in this novel,

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:11.832
this is actually the third appointment

00:24:11.832 --> 00:24:14.062
that takes place on the stroke of 12:00,

00:24:14.062 --> 00:24:16.610
the first is from "The Monk"
between the Great Mogul

00:24:16.610 --> 00:24:19.303
and Don Raymond; the second
is Catherine Moreland's

00:24:19.303 --> 00:24:22.892
frustrated appointment
with Eleanor Tilney.

00:24:22.892 --> 00:24:25.059
You know, there seems to be some type

00:24:25.059 --> 00:24:27.892
of dialogue here, because, for me,

00:24:29.169 --> 00:24:32.619
in such passages, the novel
translates the gothic villain

00:24:32.619 --> 00:24:34.811
into tormenting psychiatrist.

00:24:34.811 --> 00:24:37.623
Likewise, Bradshaw's goddess, proportion,

00:24:37.623 --> 00:24:41.703
emerges as a cruel, uncompromising deity,

00:24:41.703 --> 00:24:43.699
uncannily inflicting her own form

00:24:43.699 --> 00:24:47.223
of psychological torment
within E.P. Thompson's culture

00:24:47.223 --> 00:24:49.177
of time discipline.

00:24:49.177 --> 00:24:51.779
Septimus' final scene
finds him hiding away

00:24:51.779 --> 00:24:55.332
in the second story bedroom of
the Smiths' Bloomsbury house,

00:24:55.332 --> 00:24:57.220
panic stricken at the sudden arrival

00:24:57.220 --> 00:25:00.092
of the physician, Holmes,
who has been transformed,

00:25:00.092 --> 00:25:03.342
in his mind, into
Bradshaw's co-conspirator.

00:25:03.342 --> 00:25:05.533
Thus, the novel manipulates a familiar set

00:25:05.533 --> 00:25:08.974
of gothic coordinates,
ultimately forcing Septimus

00:25:08.974 --> 00:25:11.317
into the role of gothic heroine,

00:25:11.317 --> 00:25:14.600
there is also the
Radcliffian tone of dread,

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:16.347
Holmes was coming up the stairs,

00:25:16.347 --> 00:25:18.388
Holmes would burst open the door,

00:25:18.388 --> 00:25:21.671
Holmes would say, "In a funk, eh?"

00:25:21.671 --> 00:25:23.997
Then there is the gruesome
outcome of the scene,

00:25:23.997 --> 00:25:26.336
as Septimus commits
suicide by hurling himself

00:25:26.336 --> 00:25:29.253
from the second story window, and finally,

00:25:29.253 --> 00:25:30.711
there is the clock, as well,

00:25:30.711 --> 00:25:34.359
whose ringing interrupts
Rezia's scattered events,

00:25:34.359 --> 00:25:36.507
or reflections on the event.

00:25:36.507 --> 00:25:39.097
"The clock was striking one, two, three,

00:25:39.097 --> 00:25:41.087
"how sensible the sound was, compared

00:25:41.087 --> 00:25:44.985
"with the thumping and
whispering, like Septimus himself.

00:25:44.985 --> 00:25:48.478
"She was falling asleep, but
the clock went on striking,

00:25:48.478 --> 00:25:50.374
"four, five, six."

00:25:50.374 --> 00:25:53.205
Here the clock offers
order, and perhaps comfort

00:25:53.205 --> 00:25:56.008
within a scene consumed by chaos,

00:25:56.008 --> 00:25:58.062
but the irony is that this clock,

00:25:58.062 --> 00:26:00.974
along with the numerous
others seen within the novel,

00:26:00.974 --> 00:26:03.573
contributes to the set of gothic forces

00:26:03.573 --> 00:26:07.139
that precipitate this chaos. Thank you.

00:26:07.139 --> 00:26:09.889
(group applauds)

00:26:13.558 --> 00:26:15.641
- Thank you, Jesse. Sean?

00:26:16.891 --> 00:26:17.871
- Well, thanks, Jonathan, and thanks,

00:26:17.871 --> 00:26:21.558
all of you, for being here.
I think I may honesty say

00:26:21.558 --> 00:26:24.569
that apart from anywhere that
my wife and daughter are,

00:26:24.569 --> 00:26:26.037
there's no place that I'd rather be

00:26:26.037 --> 00:26:28.620
than The Lewis Walpole Library.

00:26:31.706 --> 00:26:34.177
I intend my remarks to
bear on what it means

00:26:34.177 --> 00:26:37.456
to take the gothic seriously,
and I mean the gothic

00:26:37.456 --> 00:26:40.126
to refer primarily to the architectural

00:26:40.126 --> 00:26:44.185
and aesthetic category, rather
than a strictly literary

00:26:44.185 --> 00:26:47.395
or narrative one, though I hope some

00:26:47.395 --> 00:26:49.102
of the connections will
emerge over the course

00:26:49.102 --> 00:26:50.269
of my remarks.

00:26:52.260 --> 00:26:56.635
Too often, we fall into a trap
that Walpole has laid for us,

00:26:56.635 --> 00:26:58.476
that is, of thinking that his lightness

00:26:58.476 --> 00:27:01.740
of style authorizes a
lightness of approach,

00:27:01.740 --> 00:27:04.109
whereas taking the gothic
seriously means treating it

00:27:04.109 --> 00:27:06.009
as a sustained, aesthetic response

00:27:06.009 --> 00:27:08.570
to mainstream enlightenment style,

00:27:08.570 --> 00:27:11.011
including the neoclassical aesthetic

00:27:11.011 --> 00:27:13.094
in arts and architecture.

00:27:14.445 --> 00:27:17.434
I mean to claim for the gothic
its status as a precursor

00:27:17.434 --> 00:27:19.874
and proving ground for complexity,

00:27:19.874 --> 00:27:21.740
which I understand is
the categorical claim

00:27:21.740 --> 00:27:24.796
that common notions or observed phenomena

00:27:24.796 --> 00:27:26.574
are the emergent effects of relationships

00:27:26.574 --> 00:27:30.741
between components that
normally remain invisible.

00:27:32.356 --> 00:27:33.318
It's a distinction that's important

00:27:33.318 --> 00:27:36.621
in cognitive philosophy,
among other places,

00:27:36.621 --> 00:27:38.648
and that's where I'm going to start.

00:27:38.648 --> 00:27:40.906
So if you were to ask someone 20 years ago

00:27:40.906 --> 00:27:43.237
to sketch out how we think,

00:27:43.237 --> 00:27:46.154
you might have seen something like,

00:27:51.734 --> 00:27:53.365
I, myself, am blocking it.

00:27:53.365 --> 00:27:55.347
(group laughs)

00:27:55.347 --> 00:27:56.256
- That's how we think?

00:27:56.256 --> 00:27:59.891
- I guess, I suppose that's true, yeah.

00:27:59.891 --> 00:28:01.521
Yeah.

00:28:01.521 --> 00:28:04.937
This is a so-called
functionalist architecture,

00:28:04.937 --> 00:28:07.239
I'm going to put some
pressure on that architecture,

00:28:07.239 --> 00:28:09.854
thinking imagined as a
series of components,

00:28:09.854 --> 00:28:13.964
sensory input is transformed
into internal representations,

00:28:13.964 --> 00:28:16.679
which are processed, looped, stored,

00:28:16.679 --> 00:28:21.551
transformed, converted into
motor, or proprioceptive output.

00:28:21.551 --> 00:28:24.468
It's based on this scheme,

00:28:24.468 --> 00:28:27.415
this is the so-called Neumann architecture

00:28:27.415 --> 00:28:30.239
from John von Neumann,
who at MIT, in the 1940s

00:28:30.239 --> 00:28:32.126
and '50s provided the basic structure

00:28:32.126 --> 00:28:35.068
for the modern microcomputer,
that's a machine

00:28:35.068 --> 00:28:38.096
that stores and transforms
representations,

00:28:38.096 --> 00:28:41.265
it has long-term memory,
short-term memory, and so on.

00:28:41.265 --> 00:28:43.264
The crucial, the crucial point is

00:28:43.264 --> 00:28:45.087
that thinking is imagined as a system

00:28:45.087 --> 00:28:47.571
of discrete, stacked components,

00:28:47.571 --> 00:28:50.815
and this is a principle
that runs all the way down,

00:28:50.815 --> 00:28:53.325
for it's also, for a system
like this is also imagined

00:28:53.325 --> 00:28:56.823
to act on discrete,
accumulated components,

00:28:56.823 --> 00:29:00.772
as the accumulator here, I
think, makes abundantly clear.

00:29:00.772 --> 00:29:02.418
And that's what we, in
cognitive theory now,

00:29:02.418 --> 00:29:06.703
what we tend to call "short-term
memory," that accumulator.

00:29:06.703 --> 00:29:08.713
Alright, if you're like
me, you can't see this

00:29:08.713 --> 00:29:12.046
without thinking of something like this.

00:29:13.926 --> 00:29:16.642
This is Chiswick House, which I choose

00:29:16.642 --> 00:29:18.338
because Walpole found it to be exemplary

00:29:18.338 --> 00:29:20.835
of neoclassical architecture,

00:29:20.835 --> 00:29:24.092
he saw it some time around 1748, 1750.

00:29:24.092 --> 00:29:27.874
Or perhaps the, this is
the Inigo Jones Gate,

00:29:27.874 --> 00:29:31.132
which is also on the
grounds of Chiswick House.

00:29:31.132 --> 00:29:33.962
It's built, like the Neumann architecture,

00:29:33.962 --> 00:29:36.801
it's built on a principle
of stacked components,

00:29:36.801 --> 00:29:40.206
right, it's a design realized in stone,

00:29:40.206 --> 00:29:43.285
its basic operative principle
is the lintel stacked

00:29:43.285 --> 00:29:46.245
on columns, or blocks stacked on blocks,

00:29:46.245 --> 00:29:49.379
architecture as the stacking of things.

00:29:49.379 --> 00:29:51.552
And I'm leaning on this
metaphor a little bit,

00:29:51.552 --> 00:29:54.062
I'm also leaning, I suppose,
on the particular magnification

00:29:54.062 --> 00:29:56.276
that images make possible,
I think I have 12 images,

00:29:56.276 --> 00:29:59.380
which I mean to stand for 12,000 words.

00:29:59.380 --> 00:30:00.805
But the links have to do with the kinds

00:30:00.805 --> 00:30:02.960
of models we'll accept, or the way

00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:05.793
that we understand things to work.

00:30:07.412 --> 00:30:09.384
So if you were to ask someone now

00:30:09.384 --> 00:30:11.391
what to sketch out a process of thinking,

00:30:11.391 --> 00:30:13.668
you might see something like this,

00:30:13.668 --> 00:30:15.201
this is a connectionist architecture,

00:30:15.201 --> 00:30:18.679
or something like this,
this is a simulation

00:30:18.679 --> 00:30:20.940
of a connectionist model
that I put together myself

00:30:20.940 --> 00:30:24.878
on Simbrain 3.0, which
is a java-based applet,

00:30:24.878 --> 00:30:27.539
which simulates connectionist
neural architectures

00:30:27.539 --> 00:30:31.231
on a formalist
microcomputer, and if you're,

00:30:31.231 --> 00:30:32.956
also if you're like me, and
I realize, at this point,

00:30:32.956 --> 00:30:34.636
I may be the only person
in the room that's like me.

00:30:34.636 --> 00:30:35.469
(group laughs)

00:30:35.469 --> 00:30:37.626
You can't think of this without thinking

00:30:37.626 --> 00:30:39.715
of something like this, right,

00:30:39.715 --> 00:30:41.103
this is King's College Chapel,

00:30:41.103 --> 00:30:43.483
and it's an example of the gothic,

00:30:43.483 --> 00:30:48.237
or this, which is its ceiling,
and it's also the place

00:30:48.237 --> 00:30:52.898
that Walpole claimed he first
lost his heart to the gothic.

00:30:52.898 --> 00:30:56.118
There was a tradition
repeated by Walpole himself

00:30:56.118 --> 00:30:59.491
that Christopher Wren
would regularly tour,

00:30:59.491 --> 00:31:02.392
regularly tour the chapel,
suggesting that he'd be happy

00:31:02.392 --> 00:31:04.173
to build a replica, if anyone could

00:31:04.173 --> 00:31:07.517
but show him where to lay the first stone.

00:31:07.517 --> 00:31:08.662
The joke's on a misunderstanding

00:31:08.662 --> 00:31:10.919
of the construction
principles of the gothic,

00:31:10.919 --> 00:31:12.795
which is not about the stacking of things,

00:31:12.795 --> 00:31:14.627
but it's about lines of force.

00:31:14.627 --> 00:31:18.266
Its fundamental principle is the rib,

00:31:18.266 --> 00:31:22.410
it's a relationship that
sustains a soaring space.

00:31:22.410 --> 00:31:24.382
This, by the way, is Walpole's homage

00:31:24.382 --> 00:31:26.799
to the King's Chapel ceiling.

00:31:28.810 --> 00:31:30.898
This is the, the fan vault ceiling

00:31:30.898 --> 00:31:33.852
in the gallery at Strawberry Hill.

00:31:33.852 --> 00:31:36.123
And the crucial thing, and this is one

00:31:36.123 --> 00:31:37.644
of the transformational moments

00:31:37.644 --> 00:31:40.699
in my own intellectual
career as a line spoken

00:31:40.699 --> 00:31:45.203
in an offhand way by Joe
Roach in a dingy room at UCLA,

00:31:45.203 --> 00:31:48.387
"No persons, or no people,
but only relationships,"

00:31:48.387 --> 00:31:50.709
which I'm extending here, no objects,

00:31:50.709 --> 00:31:52.947
but only relationships, no ideas,

00:31:52.947 --> 00:31:55.430
no thoughts, but only relationships.

00:31:55.430 --> 00:31:58.267
That, no stones here to be stacked,

00:31:58.267 --> 00:32:00.608
but only connections, not stacking,

00:32:00.608 --> 00:32:02.368
but weaving, the emergence of something

00:32:02.368 --> 00:32:04.780
where there was nothing before.

00:32:04.780 --> 00:32:09.083
In general, connectionists
align themselves,

00:32:09.083 --> 00:32:10.347
connectionists align themselves

00:32:10.347 --> 00:32:12.749
with a recent turn in the
the sciences towards complexity,

00:32:12.749 --> 00:32:14.949
and they trace it back about 50 years,

00:32:14.949 --> 00:32:18.058
to developments, especially
with increased computing power

00:32:18.058 --> 00:32:22.021
in the 1960s, '70s, and
'80s, but I'm suggesting,

00:32:22.021 --> 00:32:24.334
complexity has shadowed
enlightenment reductionism

00:32:24.334 --> 00:32:27.714
all along, it's just that we
don't take these alternatives

00:32:27.714 --> 00:32:30.964
as seriously as intellectual exercises.

00:32:38.185 --> 00:32:40.072
I'm going to wrap up,
or I'm going to sum up,

00:32:40.072 --> 00:32:42.389
I suppose, with three provocations,

00:32:42.389 --> 00:32:44.331
three, because three is the minimal number

00:32:44.331 --> 00:32:48.235
that it takes to create
a complex structure.

00:32:48.235 --> 00:32:50.833
Walpole himself saw the gothic
as the major alternative

00:32:50.833 --> 00:32:54.330
to neoclassicism, from
the stately rational order

00:32:54.330 --> 00:32:57.180
of what he called "the Grecian," or we,

00:32:57.180 --> 00:33:00.601
the national, the neoclassical, he opposed

00:33:00.601 --> 00:33:03.506
what he called "the
liberty of the gothic."

00:33:03.506 --> 00:33:05.320
And while most of us
would be likely to think

00:33:05.320 --> 00:33:07.750
of the origins of
architecture as a history

00:33:07.750 --> 00:33:11.917
of the stacking of stones,
Walpole himself was quite clear,

00:33:12.827 --> 00:33:15.981
the first architectural
gesture was the weaving

00:33:15.981 --> 00:33:18.761
of branches to form a roof, right, woven,

00:33:18.761 --> 00:33:20.743
rather than stacked,
and I see that repeated

00:33:20.743 --> 00:33:23.576
in the fan vault ceiling.

00:33:23.576 --> 00:33:26.123
He has, in other words,
flipped the paradigm,

00:33:26.123 --> 00:33:30.253
it's relationships are prior to objects.

00:33:30.253 --> 00:33:33.503
Two, it's about ornament,
right, it's a part

00:33:33.503 --> 00:33:35.778
of the legacy of the
Enlightenment to fetishize forms

00:33:35.778 --> 00:33:37.749
which seems to follow functions,

00:33:37.749 --> 00:33:39.760
the evidently minimalist stacked forms

00:33:39.760 --> 00:33:41.362
of the neoclassical aesthetic would seem

00:33:41.362 --> 00:33:44.010
to fit this criterion, but a better way

00:33:44.010 --> 00:33:46.873
to think about form is as
the outward manifestation

00:33:46.873 --> 00:33:50.123
of design principles, right,
aesthetics as a signal

00:33:50.123 --> 00:33:52.866
of the way we imagine
things to be put together.

00:33:52.866 --> 00:33:55.805
In this light, it's certainly significant

00:33:55.805 --> 00:33:57.332
that buildings like Chiswick House,

00:33:57.332 --> 00:33:59.332
or the Inigo Jones Gate,

00:34:01.306 --> 00:34:02.139
here,

00:34:04.224 --> 00:34:07.368
are brick that are then covered in stucco,

00:34:07.368 --> 00:34:10.368
which is tooled to look like blocks,

00:34:11.658 --> 00:34:13.523
which makes its principles manifest

00:34:13.523 --> 00:34:15.814
in precisely the same
way that Walpole did,

00:34:15.814 --> 00:34:18.854
whose gothic ornamentation
was historically,

00:34:18.854 --> 00:34:21.771
and I think unfairly been lambasted

00:34:22.946 --> 00:34:25.400
for being made out of papier-mache,

00:34:25.400 --> 00:34:28.796
as somehow too ornamental,
or perhaps not gothic enough.

00:34:28.796 --> 00:34:30.138
And also, in this light,
I think it's important

00:34:30.138 --> 00:34:33.010
that repeatedly we see
Strawberry Hill framed

00:34:33.010 --> 00:34:36.657
by these kind of vegetable arches.

00:34:36.657 --> 00:34:38.727
My final point, I've
been arguing all along

00:34:38.727 --> 00:34:40.944
that functionalism, like the neoclassical,

00:34:40.944 --> 00:34:43.056
implies certain habits of thought.

00:34:43.056 --> 00:34:46.422
The neoclassical edifice
is designed, it's held as

00:34:46.422 --> 00:34:50.539
an internal representation
before being realized in stone,

00:34:50.539 --> 00:34:52.508
indeed, a representation often developed

00:34:52.508 --> 00:34:55.287
by one man, Christopher
Wren, for instance.

00:34:55.287 --> 00:34:58.900
This is why it's often
associated with reason,

00:34:58.900 --> 00:35:01.778
but the gothic can have
no prior representation,

00:35:01.778 --> 00:35:04.883
it's surely significant that
each major gothic revival was

00:35:04.883 --> 00:35:08.333
a collaborative effort, John Ruskin,

00:35:08.333 --> 00:35:12.500
in a famously vexed love triangle
in the Scottish highlands,

00:35:13.372 --> 00:35:17.384
William Morris in a circle
of students at Oxford,

00:35:17.384 --> 00:35:18.952
and, of course, Horace Walpole,

00:35:18.952 --> 00:35:21.796
and the Committee of
Taste at Strawberry Hill.

00:35:21.796 --> 00:35:25.117
The gothic, in other
words, as a negotiation,

00:35:25.117 --> 00:35:27.370
nobody designed Canterbury Cathedral,

00:35:27.370 --> 00:35:29.402
it evolved in partnerships
between generations

00:35:29.402 --> 00:35:31.738
of architects, builders, and craftsmen,

00:35:31.738 --> 00:35:33.076
and this is why the style appealed

00:35:33.076 --> 00:35:35.336
to Ruskin as a political program,

00:35:35.336 --> 00:35:38.683
and it's why it appealed to
Walpole as an aesthetic one,

00:35:38.683 --> 00:35:40.527
because it registers a design principle,

00:35:40.527 --> 00:35:42.135
which is relational, which is, to say,

00:35:42.135 --> 00:35:44.893
connectionist, through and through.

00:35:44.893 --> 00:35:46.954
The connectionist school
is part of a recent turn

00:35:46.954 --> 00:35:49.617
to complexity, and the sciences
generally pitches itself

00:35:49.617 --> 00:35:51.696
as a radical alternative to the legacy

00:35:51.696 --> 00:35:53.514
of Enlightenment reductionism,

00:35:53.514 --> 00:35:56.271
an alternative roughly 40 or 50 years old.

00:35:56.271 --> 00:35:58.573
But as I've been suggesting,
complexity has existed

00:35:58.573 --> 00:36:01.525
alongside functionalist
explanations all along,

00:36:01.525 --> 00:36:03.048
it's only that until we're willing

00:36:03.048 --> 00:36:06.707
to accept ornament as essential,
rather than superficial,

00:36:06.707 --> 00:36:08.964
we're unlikely to see the
gothic as anything other

00:36:08.964 --> 00:36:11.987
than a self-indulgent
aesthetic experiment.

00:36:11.987 --> 00:36:14.178
Put differently,
establishing an alternative

00:36:14.178 --> 00:36:16.352
to the burden of Enlightenment,

00:36:16.352 --> 00:36:19.323
the burden of the Enlightenment
in modern thought begins,

00:36:19.323 --> 00:36:21.747
I'm suggesting, by taking the gothic

00:36:21.747 --> 00:36:24.814
at least as seriously as
we take the neoclassical,

00:36:24.814 --> 00:36:26.610
including architectural projects,

00:36:26.610 --> 00:36:30.026
like Chiswick House, but also
the functionalist paradigm

00:36:30.026 --> 00:36:32.044
to which it gives rise.

00:36:32.044 --> 00:36:34.794
(group applauds)

00:36:40.838 --> 00:36:42.984
- Thank you, Sean. Cindy?

00:36:42.984 --> 00:36:47.506
- I have a handout that
actually involved cutting,

00:36:47.506 --> 00:36:50.755
and scotch tape, and magic markers,

00:36:50.755 --> 00:36:54.345
kind of a different take,

00:36:54.345 --> 00:36:57.355
I, too, want to thank our hosts here

00:36:57.355 --> 00:37:02.258
for what Joe has called
"a Country House weekend,"

00:37:02.258 --> 00:37:06.425
and I'm going to begin with
a well-known Walpole's dream,

00:37:08.839 --> 00:37:11.256
which is quoted in two parts.

00:37:14.207 --> 00:37:16.573
The genesis of Otranto,
of course, as we all know,

00:37:16.573 --> 00:37:19.857
is when, as Walpole says,
"I waked one morning

00:37:19.857 --> 00:37:22.546
"in the beginning of
last June from a dream

00:37:22.546 --> 00:37:25.546
"of which all I could recover
was that I had thought myself

00:37:25.546 --> 00:37:28.251
"in an ancient castle,
a very natural dream

00:37:28.251 --> 00:37:32.115
"for a head, filled like
mine, with gothic story,

00:37:32.115 --> 00:37:33.648
"and that on the uppermost banister

00:37:33.648 --> 00:37:37.368
"of a great staircase, I saw
a gigantic hand in armor.

00:37:37.368 --> 00:37:40.083
"In the evening, I sat
down and began to write,

00:37:40.083 --> 00:37:41.249
"without knowing, in the least,

00:37:41.249 --> 00:37:43.585
"what I intended to say or relate.

00:37:43.585 --> 00:37:46.649
"Work grew on my hands,
and I grew fond of it,

00:37:46.649 --> 00:37:49.169
"add that I was very glad
to think of anything rather

00:37:49.169 --> 00:37:53.497
"than politics," huzzah
for the Walpolapalooza.

00:37:53.497 --> 00:37:55.395
(group laughs)

00:37:55.395 --> 00:37:58.565
I have to say that when
Jonathan informed us

00:37:58.565 --> 00:38:01.732
that this would be videoed, recorded,

00:38:02.607 --> 00:38:04.684
I said, "Thank you very much, Jonathan,"

00:38:04.684 --> 00:38:07.480
and then I promptly had a dream myself,

00:38:07.480 --> 00:38:10.942
in which, we were all
gathered here in sort of pews,

00:38:10.942 --> 00:38:13.448
and Jonathan was walking among us

00:38:13.448 --> 00:38:16.781
in 18th century waistcoats, and he said,

00:38:18.044 --> 00:38:20.788
"Right, we will proceed in order,

00:38:20.788 --> 00:38:23.321
"alphabetically, by costume."

00:38:23.321 --> 00:38:24.742
(group laughs)

00:38:24.742 --> 00:38:27.900
And this being my dream,
of course I had no costume.

00:38:27.900 --> 00:38:30.483
(group laughs)

00:38:32.372 --> 00:38:34.106
So, but I'll get to the second part

00:38:34.106 --> 00:38:36.292
of Walpole's quotation in a minute.

00:38:36.292 --> 00:38:39.648
My comments today, come, actually come out

00:38:39.648 --> 00:38:43.201
of my classroom experience this semester

00:38:43.201 --> 00:38:45.811
in reading "Otranto," I'm
teaching a first year course

00:38:45.811 --> 00:38:48.076
on the art of reading and writing,

00:38:48.076 --> 00:38:51.376
and it has a gothic theme,
from the 18th century

00:38:51.376 --> 00:38:53.284
to the 20th, from "Castle of Otranto"

00:38:53.284 --> 00:38:56.519
to "The Haunting of Hill
House" and Stephen King.

00:38:56.519 --> 00:39:00.012
And so we started with "Otranto," and I had,

00:39:00.012 --> 00:39:03.955
the required edition was
Lewis' "Oxford World's Classics"

00:39:03.955 --> 00:39:06.483
with Cleary's notes and introduction.

00:39:06.483 --> 00:39:09.291
And I was having the students
read things out loud,

00:39:09.291 --> 00:39:11.915
assigning them different characters,

00:39:11.915 --> 00:39:14.926
and it didn't go very smoothly,

00:39:14.926 --> 00:39:17.382
because they kind of kept
tripping over each other,

00:39:17.382 --> 00:39:20.609
landing in each other's dialogue space.

00:39:20.609 --> 00:39:21.797
And then they complained,

00:39:21.797 --> 00:39:25.647
"But there aren't any quotation marks,"

00:39:25.647 --> 00:39:26.907
although, then some people said,

00:39:26.907 --> 00:39:29.434
"Mine has quotation marks,"
and someone else said,

00:39:29.434 --> 00:39:31.906
"Well, mine does," so we
started comparing editions,

00:39:31.906 --> 00:39:34.180
and we talked about the
history of quotation marks

00:39:34.180 --> 00:39:36.479
for a little bit, and how, in the middle

00:39:36.479 --> 00:39:39.305
of the 18th century,
people often used dashes

00:39:39.305 --> 00:39:42.517
to indicate the next speaker,

00:39:42.517 --> 00:39:46.200
although Walpole isn't very
consistent in that, either.

00:39:46.200 --> 00:39:49.002
And then I started
thinking, I started kind

00:39:49.002 --> 00:39:51.378
of paying attention to
the sentences again,

00:39:51.378 --> 00:39:56.337
and this comes out of my book
that's coming out this spring,

00:39:56.337 --> 00:39:59.132
includes a lot of
attention to the typography

00:39:59.132 --> 00:40:02.087
of the page, and the texture of prose.

00:40:02.087 --> 00:40:06.138
So lots of typographical
interest going on here,

00:40:06.138 --> 00:40:09.727
as well as history of
prose rhythms and things,

00:40:09.727 --> 00:40:13.894
general landscape of text,
as well as landscape of land.

00:40:16.267 --> 00:40:19.316
And it struck me that in a, at first,

00:40:19.316 --> 00:40:22.286
it seemed fanciful, but
then I started following it

00:40:22.286 --> 00:40:26.036
through, Walpole's syntax
is, itself, gothic.

00:40:27.449 --> 00:40:30.705
The sentences follow their
own weird little corridors,

00:40:30.705 --> 00:40:32.877
you turn a corner, and you
bump into something else,

00:40:32.877 --> 00:40:34.600
you have to look back over your shoulder

00:40:34.600 --> 00:40:37.933
to see who was talking, so I decided to,

00:40:39.092 --> 00:40:41.425
you know, work through this.

00:40:43.474 --> 00:40:44.925
You know, of course we could say,

00:40:44.925 --> 00:40:48.671
along with our dear Mr. Lewis, who said,

00:40:48.671 --> 00:40:51.519
"I marvel how such a lucid
and entertaining writer

00:40:51.519 --> 00:40:54.123
"as Horace Walpole could have written

00:40:54.123 --> 00:40:56.541
"so confused and clumsy a book."

00:40:56.541 --> 00:40:58.042
(group laughs)

00:40:58.042 --> 00:41:01.466
But I will work with Sean here and go

00:41:01.466 --> 00:41:04.517
for the importance of design, the effect,

00:41:04.517 --> 00:41:07.225
for me, of "Otranto," has
always been the effect

00:41:07.225 --> 00:41:10.687
of design in different ways,
and the more you push it,

00:41:10.687 --> 00:41:13.354
the more it hangs together, and,

00:41:14.407 --> 00:41:16.824
of course, Lewis himself was,

00:41:21.239 --> 00:41:24.150
described some of the works
that came out of the press,

00:41:24.150 --> 00:41:26.944
his Strawberry Hill Press, of,

00:41:26.944 --> 00:41:30.747
"guilty of the beautiful
negligence of a gentleman,"

00:41:30.747 --> 00:41:31.997
but he was also

00:41:35.687 --> 00:41:38.345
not very convincingly self-deprecatory,

00:41:38.345 --> 00:41:42.575
and certainly in his
sixth edition of "Otranto,"

00:41:42.575 --> 00:41:47.234
printed by Bodoni and Parma,
he had so many corrections

00:41:47.234 --> 00:41:49.905
that it delayed the publication.

00:41:49.905 --> 00:41:52.405
So he is, was a man of detail.

00:41:55.659 --> 00:41:58.159
So the rest of that quotation,

00:42:03.805 --> 00:42:06.505
"In short, I was so
engrossed with my tale,

00:42:06.505 --> 00:42:09.105
"which I completed in
less than two months,

00:42:09.105 --> 00:42:11.987
"that one evening I wrote from
the time I had drunk my tea,

00:42:11.987 --> 00:42:14.712
"about six o'clock, til
half an hour after 1:00

00:42:14.712 --> 00:42:17.910
"in the morning, when my hand
and fingers were so weary

00:42:17.910 --> 00:42:21.835
"that I could not hold the
pen to finish the sentence,

00:42:21.835 --> 00:42:24.688
"but left Matilda and Isabella talking

00:42:24.688 --> 00:42:27.208
"in the middle of a paragraph."

00:42:27.208 --> 00:42:29.826
So my remarks today
are looking at the ends

00:42:29.826 --> 00:42:33.493
of sentences, and the
middles of paragraphs.

00:42:35.055 --> 00:42:38.369
Cleary's note to the
epigraph of "Otranto" tells us

00:42:38.369 --> 00:42:40.437
that Walpole reverses the meaning,

00:42:40.437 --> 00:42:43.515
to say that, "Nevertheless,
head and foot are assigned

00:42:43.515 --> 00:42:47.348
"to a single shape," I
think the sentences in,

00:42:48.322 --> 00:42:52.405
Walpole's syntactics were
in that shape, as well.

00:42:58.625 --> 00:43:00.375
So, with the handout.

00:43:03.436 --> 00:43:07.603
The blue marks my looking
at the ends of sentences.

00:43:09.624 --> 00:43:13.826
In the very first sentence, the
very first two sentences end

00:43:13.826 --> 00:43:17.743
with Matilda, and, of
course, she ends up being

00:43:22.748 --> 00:43:25.353
the prominent one, in so many ways,

00:43:25.353 --> 00:43:27.603
and that particular syntax,

00:43:30.557 --> 00:43:34.596
we might say that the
dispositionally challenged Conrad

00:43:34.596 --> 00:43:38.763
is syntactically obliterated
in those two sentences.

00:43:40.008 --> 00:43:42.310
The one on, the bottom of the paragraph,

00:43:42.310 --> 00:43:46.889
on the top of page 25, so
the upper right hand corner,

00:43:46.889 --> 00:43:49.580
left her in total
darkness, one of the first,

00:43:49.580 --> 00:43:54.343
you know, gothic moves of
cliffhanging paragraphs,

00:43:54.343 --> 00:43:57.343
inventing the tropes as we go along.

00:43:58.563 --> 00:44:01.620
On the flip side, you know, the events,

00:44:01.620 --> 00:44:05.203
charging in, "It is my
child, my Theodore."

00:44:07.165 --> 00:44:10.208
Lewis himself, again, in another work,

00:44:10.208 --> 00:44:12.750
trying to figure out
the appeal of "Otranto,"

00:44:12.750 --> 00:44:16.635
he said, "It must be because
something is always happening,

00:44:16.635 --> 00:44:19.903
"the end of every dialogue
ends in an explosion,"

00:44:19.903 --> 00:44:21.306
well, the end of every paragraph,

00:44:21.306 --> 00:44:23.515
and in some ways, the
end of every sentence,

00:44:23.515 --> 00:44:27.682
ends up in something
happening one way or another.

00:44:31.198 --> 00:44:32.531
The, the passage

00:44:36.919 --> 00:44:39.822
with Matilda sinking,
it's Manfred gliding

00:44:39.822 --> 00:44:44.678
to the death of his daughter,
and then the last sentence

00:44:44.678 --> 00:44:46.345
of the book is just,

00:44:49.783 --> 00:44:51.313
not so much an explosion,

00:44:51.313 --> 00:44:53.753
but this limp thing we're left with,

00:44:53.753 --> 00:44:55.831
sort of the anti-explosion.

00:44:55.831 --> 00:44:58.321
But more interesting, to me, well, okay,

00:44:58.321 --> 00:45:01.257
the orange is other interesting things

00:45:01.257 --> 00:45:04.257
that happened syntactically in here.

00:45:06.274 --> 00:45:08.870
41 through 43 is one of those places

00:45:08.870 --> 00:45:12.123
where the gothic starts
making its own textual,

00:45:12.123 --> 00:45:16.407
subterranean spaces, so
Manfred disappears for awhile,

00:45:16.407 --> 00:45:19.125
and comes back, and this
is elaborated beautifully

00:45:19.125 --> 00:45:22.131
in "The Monk" and "Mysteries of Udolpho."

00:45:22.131 --> 00:45:25.744
And then gliding softly
between another sense

00:45:25.744 --> 00:45:27.577
of sort of form and content,

00:45:27.577 --> 00:45:30.293
there's a gliding going
on in the paragraph.

00:45:30.293 --> 00:45:33.492
But most interesting
to me is those moments

00:45:33.492 --> 00:45:36.149
where one person's speaking lands

00:45:36.149 --> 00:45:39.390
in another person's territory,

00:45:39.390 --> 00:45:43.101
and that's kind of, it
struck me like Sedgwick

00:45:43.101 --> 00:45:47.645
on identity and surface,
where personal identity

00:45:47.645 --> 00:45:51.565
in the gothic is at no
moment inherent in one,

00:45:51.565 --> 00:45:55.944
but applicable is applied
only from outside,

00:45:55.944 --> 00:45:59.719
après coup, and by a process
of visual, a simulation,

00:45:59.719 --> 00:46:03.124
or seeing as, and in "Otranto," your eye has

00:46:03.124 --> 00:46:05.819
to be pretty quick and skilled,

00:46:05.819 --> 00:46:07.062
you have to kind of be familiar

00:46:07.062 --> 00:46:10.160
with the text, and the passages,

00:46:10.160 --> 00:46:13.021
or the passageways of the prose

00:46:13.021 --> 00:46:15.514
in order to navigate the turns

00:46:15.514 --> 00:46:17.877
and assimilate the identities.

00:46:17.877 --> 00:46:20.492
Look on the bottom of the front page,

00:46:20.492 --> 00:46:25.005
on the left hand side is that
scene alluded to earlier,

00:46:25.005 --> 00:46:28.366
Jaquez, and Diego, and Manfred all,

00:46:28.366 --> 00:46:31.099
the babble is syntactically reproduced,

00:46:31.099 --> 00:46:33.371
it's a mess, on the right hand side,

00:46:33.371 --> 00:46:36.679
facing it, is Henry Morley's 1886 edition,

00:46:36.679 --> 00:46:40.694
which is an example of
beautiful Victorian engineering.

00:46:40.694 --> 00:46:41.527
(group laughs)

00:46:41.527 --> 00:46:43.913
Everybody's identity is
carefully sorted out,

00:46:43.913 --> 00:46:47.128
not just by quotation
marks, but in paragraphs,

00:46:47.128 --> 00:46:51.295
we make no mistake, whereas
in the Walpole itself,

00:46:52.261 --> 00:46:54.440
we make the mistake all the time,

00:46:54.440 --> 00:46:57.167
it's kind of the opposite
of the primary objects

00:46:57.167 --> 00:47:01.144
in the text, the identities
of the characters are,

00:47:01.144 --> 00:47:03.593
you know, slide between each other,

00:47:03.593 --> 00:47:07.919
and through the sentences,
so the game I was playing

00:47:07.919 --> 00:47:10.754
with myself, and then with my students,

00:47:10.754 --> 00:47:13.972
the yellow doesn't come
here through so much,

00:47:13.972 --> 00:47:17.965
but it's a, it's a kind
of linguistic game.

00:47:17.965 --> 00:47:21.696
"I fear no bad angel, and
have offended no good one,"

00:47:21.696 --> 00:47:24.975
and then that territory, you
know, he could be saying,

00:47:24.975 --> 00:47:27.662
"Your behavior has been
so gracious to me,"

00:47:27.662 --> 00:47:31.717
or, you know, but it's not, is above your seeming

00:47:31.717 --> 00:47:33.567
well, somewhere we know that's not

00:47:33.567 --> 00:47:35.307
Theodore's speaking,
it's going to be Manfred,

00:47:35.307 --> 00:47:36.724
and then it says,

00:47:40.348 --> 00:47:42.330
left-hand side, on the second page,

00:47:42.330 --> 00:47:45.312
"They seem to inquire, in
the eyes of their Lord,

00:47:45.312 --> 00:47:48.119
"what they ought to feel,
surprise, doubt, tenderness,

00:47:48.119 --> 00:47:52.298
"respect, succeed each
other in the countenance

00:47:52.298 --> 00:47:57.145
"of the youth," oops,
"depart, it cannot be known

00:47:57.145 --> 00:48:00.488
"that I assisted thy flight,
swear by the saints above."

00:48:00.488 --> 00:48:04.225
Well, no, Matilda isn't
going to be swearing, okay?

00:48:04.225 --> 00:48:08.505
"Ah, rest assured that no
suspicion can alight on me,"

00:48:08.505 --> 00:48:11.695
she says, "give me thy beauteous hand."

00:48:11.695 --> 00:48:13.413
No, Matilda's not going to be asking

00:48:13.413 --> 00:48:15.746
for anyone's beauteous hand.

00:48:17.369 --> 00:48:19.536
"There is not a sentiment
engraven in my heart

00:48:19.536 --> 00:48:22.281
"that does not venerate you and yours,

00:48:22.281 --> 00:48:26.277
"the grace and generosity you've
exhibited to your people."

00:48:26.277 --> 00:48:30.658
No, "the grace and fervor with
which Theodore uttered this,"

00:48:30.658 --> 00:48:33.908
see, all of these not marked by dashes.

00:48:37.374 --> 00:48:39.019
"What deed of Manfred authorizes you

00:48:39.019 --> 00:48:41.241
"to treat him as a murderer and assassin,

00:48:41.241 --> 00:48:44.016
"thou virtuous, gentle maiden," I think,

00:48:44.016 --> 00:48:46.518
"have you such thoughts,

00:48:46.518 --> 00:48:50.232
"Manfred will never permit our union."

00:48:50.232 --> 00:48:52.417
And then it might be,
whoever's responding,

00:48:52.417 --> 00:48:56.213
"No, I shall take care of everything,

00:48:56.213 --> 00:49:00.487
"oh, thou of the beauteous
hand," no, it's Manfred.

00:49:00.487 --> 00:49:04.114
And then I guess I just want to end

00:49:04.114 --> 00:49:07.564
with the idea that in the middle

00:49:07.564 --> 00:49:11.731
of that last paragraph that
ends with the limp Theodore,

00:49:13.295 --> 00:49:16.386
we've got Isabella and
his dear Matilda mixed

00:49:16.386 --> 00:49:19.577
in the middle of the paragraph,

00:49:19.577 --> 00:49:21.729
so that's my gothic excursion.

00:49:21.729 --> 00:49:24.479
(group applauds)

00:49:28.662 --> 00:49:30.995
- Thank you, Cindy. Eugenia?

00:49:32.465 --> 00:49:33.798
- So, like Sean,

00:49:35.454 --> 00:49:37.948
I have been thinking a lot lately

00:49:37.948 --> 00:49:42.115
about what it would mean to
take Walpole's gothic seriously,

00:49:44.429 --> 00:49:45.926
but I've been thinking specifically

00:49:45.926 --> 00:49:49.618
about what it would mean to take Walpole's

00:49:49.618 --> 00:49:53.763
and "The Castle of Otranto's"
funniness seriously,

00:49:53.763 --> 00:49:55.951
which means I've really
been thinking about

00:49:55.951 --> 00:49:59.431
what it would mean to take the novels,

00:49:59.431 --> 00:50:01.174
or the novellas' refusal

00:50:01.174 --> 00:50:04.499
to take certain things
seriously, seriously.

00:50:04.499 --> 00:50:05.357
(group laughs)

00:50:05.357 --> 00:50:08.940
And that's what my, my
talk today is about.

00:50:09.839 --> 00:50:13.006
I was, started on this path of thought

00:50:14.012 --> 00:50:18.233
by reading Cindy's section
on "The Castle of Otranto"

00:50:18.233 --> 00:50:21.787
in "The Prose of Things," which
is such a marvelous reading

00:50:21.787 --> 00:50:25.763
of this novel, and so I'm, it's both fun

00:50:25.763 --> 00:50:29.130
to be able to do this right
after Cindy has presented,

00:50:29.130 --> 00:50:30.647
and maybe a little bit embarrassing,

00:50:30.647 --> 00:50:32.725
but my paper is also kind of a love letter

00:50:32.725 --> 00:50:35.079
to your section on "Otranto,"

00:50:35.079 --> 00:50:38.686
in that book, so thank you very much.

00:50:38.686 --> 00:50:41.985
So my talk begins with a narrative

00:50:41.985 --> 00:50:44.771
of the haphazard beginnings
of my current project

00:50:44.771 --> 00:50:48.480
on funniness, which was
routed in various ways

00:50:48.480 --> 00:50:52.336
through Walpole, and "The
Castle of Otranto" at its start,

00:50:52.336 --> 00:50:55.715
and I was enabled in
this kind of odd start

00:50:55.715 --> 00:50:59.244
to a book on funniness by
reading Cindy's amazing section

00:50:59.244 --> 00:51:01.969
on "Otranto" in "The Prose of Things,"

00:51:01.969 --> 00:51:05.621
which reads "Otranto" as a
kind of Swiftian satire

00:51:05.621 --> 00:51:07.584
of the disproportionate significance

00:51:07.584 --> 00:51:11.738
of objects in modern life,
whose comedy is premised

00:51:11.738 --> 00:51:16.255
on things literally being
thrown out of proportion, right?

00:51:16.255 --> 00:51:20.914
Giant helmet is probably
the foremost example.

00:51:20.914 --> 00:51:23.830
Cindy's reading confirmed
what I'd always felt,

00:51:23.830 --> 00:51:26.375
that "Otranto" was a deeply funny book,

00:51:26.375 --> 00:51:29.013
and not just by accident,
or maybe by accident,

00:51:29.013 --> 00:51:32.608
but not just as a kind
of effect of failing

00:51:32.608 --> 00:51:35.646
to be funny, I mean, failing to be scary.

00:51:35.646 --> 00:51:39.327
I was distracted with
how funny the helmet is,

00:51:39.327 --> 00:51:42.685
in particular, with its constantly waving

00:51:42.685 --> 00:51:46.184
proportionable quantity
of black feathers, right,

00:51:46.184 --> 00:51:47.994
which Cindy calls, at one point,

00:51:47.994 --> 00:51:51.678
the feathers "less
inherently dignified objects

00:51:51.678 --> 00:51:54.964
"than helmets and swords," which means

00:51:54.964 --> 00:51:57.798
that a lot of this project
has been me thinking

00:51:57.798 --> 00:51:59.964
about the relative
dignity of different kinds

00:51:59.964 --> 00:52:00.797
of objects.

00:52:00.797 --> 00:52:02.380
(group laughs)

00:52:02.380 --> 00:52:06.083
A funniness only compounded
by the image of it,

00:52:06.083 --> 00:52:08.962
the helmet, having squashed Conrad,

00:52:08.962 --> 00:52:11.946
the feathers waving
ominously over the bleeding,

00:52:11.946 --> 00:52:15.679
mangled remains, and
maybe, to a lesser extent,

00:52:15.679 --> 00:52:19.634
by the image of Theodore,
trapped under the helmet.

00:52:19.634 --> 00:52:22.741
So thinking about the helmet in relation

00:52:22.741 --> 00:52:25.479
to these bodies that kind
of get positioned around it,

00:52:25.479 --> 00:52:27.513
I started to ask research questions.

00:52:27.513 --> 00:52:30.878
Why is this helmet so funny,
why does it get funnier

00:52:30.878 --> 00:52:34.662
in proximity to death, is it
because I'm a horrible person?

00:52:34.662 --> 00:52:35.976
(group laughs)

00:52:35.976 --> 00:52:40.066
Or is it doing something,
is it doing something?

00:52:40.066 --> 00:52:44.223
I spent a lot of time
thinking about the tableau

00:52:44.223 --> 00:52:47.026
of the scene of Conrad's death,

00:52:47.026 --> 00:52:50.105
with the same engrossment
with which Manfred,

00:52:50.105 --> 00:52:52.921
of course, views the
single, overwhelming detail

00:52:52.921 --> 00:52:55.453
of the helmet, right, so he's, you know,

00:52:55.453 --> 00:52:57.014
he's transfixed by this helmet,

00:52:57.014 --> 00:53:00.706
and I spent a lot of time just
trying to imagine the scene

00:53:00.706 --> 00:53:03.676
of Manfred looking at the
helmet, and the feathers waving,

00:53:03.676 --> 00:53:06.038
and the bleeding, mangled remains kind of,

00:53:06.038 --> 00:53:09.538
you know, doing whatever they do, messily.

00:53:11.115 --> 00:53:13.901
The moment began to strike me, the moment

00:53:13.901 --> 00:53:17.698
of Conrad's death, as a very funny parody

00:53:17.698 --> 00:53:21.875
of still life, a scene
of life suddenly stilled,

00:53:21.875 --> 00:53:24.027
one life because it is crushed,

00:53:24.027 --> 00:53:26.710
and one because it is transfixed,

00:53:26.710 --> 00:53:29.886
and the constantly
agitated feathers seemed,

00:53:29.886 --> 00:53:33.248
in a kind of double metonymic movement

00:53:33.248 --> 00:53:35.448
to appropriate the life
that had been taken

00:53:35.448 --> 00:53:38.986
from the two human
figures in this tableau,

00:53:38.986 --> 00:53:41.647
and transfer that life, inappropriately,

00:53:41.647 --> 00:53:44.083
to the helmet, which remains animated

00:53:44.083 --> 00:53:47.525
throughout the tale, so
I began to think a lot

00:53:47.525 --> 00:53:49.853
about feathers, this is the very beginning

00:53:49.853 --> 00:53:52.183
of a research project,
so just remember this.

00:53:52.183 --> 00:53:53.855
I began to think a lot about feathers,

00:53:53.855 --> 00:53:57.398
about dead things,
particularly dead birds,

00:53:57.398 --> 00:53:59.866
which is where feather come from,

00:53:59.866 --> 00:54:02.978
whether they're funny
or not, not in reality,

00:54:02.978 --> 00:54:04.444
of course, 'cause that would be horrible,

00:54:04.444 --> 00:54:06.374
but in representation.

00:54:06.374 --> 00:54:09.264
And right around this time I received,

00:54:09.264 --> 00:54:11.474
fortuitously, both a sabbatical,

00:54:11.474 --> 00:54:14.637
and a sizeable research grant
from the Canadian government.

00:54:14.637 --> 00:54:16.930
Thank you, SSHRC, so in pursuit

00:54:16.930 --> 00:54:19.903
of the secrets to why things are funny,

00:54:19.903 --> 00:54:23.892
I went to Europe armed with a
copy of "The Castle of Otranto,"

00:54:23.892 --> 00:54:26.842
and spent three solid
weeks looking at paintings

00:54:26.842 --> 00:54:31.275
of dead animals, that was the
beginning of this project.

00:54:31.275 --> 00:54:33.807
I also went to the newly
renovated Strawberry Hill

00:54:33.807 --> 00:54:37.827
for the first time, and
that was pretty incredible.

00:54:37.827 --> 00:54:39.797
I was very distracted by my mission

00:54:39.797 --> 00:54:43.939
to understand the image of dead animals,

00:54:43.939 --> 00:54:46.055
dead creatures, and birds, in particular,

00:54:46.055 --> 00:54:49.752
so I took a photograph of every
single bird I came across,

00:54:49.752 --> 00:54:53.054
in like the ornamentation
of Strawberry Hill,

00:54:53.054 --> 00:54:56.240
and I was surprised to
find there a lot of them.

00:54:56.240 --> 00:54:59.687
I also thought a lot about hybrid objects,

00:54:59.687 --> 00:55:03.133
or what I, I sometimes think
of as confused objects,

00:55:03.133 --> 00:55:06.861
I mean, confused is a
term that the novel uses,

00:55:06.861 --> 00:55:10.135
and I love the quotation from Lewis

00:55:10.135 --> 00:55:14.942
that describes the tale as,
as "clumsy and confused."

00:55:14.942 --> 00:55:18.386
I come back to clumsiness,
kind of towards the end here.

00:55:18.386 --> 00:55:22.730
But confused objects combining,
like the giant helmet,

00:55:22.730 --> 00:55:26.397
creaturely detritus,
and man-made materials,

00:55:27.753 --> 00:55:31.935
and that is how I became
interested to distraction

00:55:31.935 --> 00:55:35.103
with nautilus cups,
does everyone here know

00:55:35.103 --> 00:55:37.308
what a nautilus cup is?

00:55:37.308 --> 00:55:39.554
I mean, show of hands,
anyone not know what a?

00:55:39.554 --> 00:55:41.535
So there, you've, I'm
sure you've seen one,

00:55:41.535 --> 00:55:43.449
but I'm not sure it registered with you.

00:55:43.449 --> 00:55:46.037
But there are these
renaissance objects made

00:55:46.037 --> 00:55:48.697
from the shell of the chambered nautilus,

00:55:48.697 --> 00:55:50.025
so that's this, you know, sea creature,

00:55:50.025 --> 00:55:52.103
and it has that, that spiral shell,

00:55:52.103 --> 00:55:54.881
but it's that large shell,
it's about this big,

00:55:54.881 --> 00:55:58.199
and the shells would
be polished, you know,

00:55:58.199 --> 00:56:02.808
so that you can, the outer,
the outer layer is taken off

00:56:02.808 --> 00:56:04.646
to reveal the mother-of-pearl,

00:56:04.646 --> 00:56:07.475
they're sometimes engraved,
but then they would be mounted

00:56:07.475 --> 00:56:12.463
on these really elaborate kind
of, like stems, and braces,

00:56:12.463 --> 00:56:16.769
to turn them into kind of,
you know, collectibles.

00:56:16.769 --> 00:56:18.574
So I was interested in one, in particular,

00:56:18.574 --> 00:56:22.748
that Walpole owned, and kept
in the great north bedchamber

00:56:22.748 --> 00:56:25.444
at Strawberry Hill, it's
now in the Prinsenhof Museum

00:56:25.444 --> 00:56:27.367
in Delft, although I think
there's a possibility

00:56:27.367 --> 00:56:29.782
it may be coming to, it may be coming

00:56:29.782 --> 00:56:33.421
to Yale next year for an exhibition.

00:56:33.421 --> 00:56:36.760
So just a few words about
nautilus cups, just a few.

00:56:36.760 --> 00:56:38.365
So the nautilus cup, in which the shell

00:56:38.365 --> 00:56:39.773
of the chambered nautilus is mounted

00:56:39.773 --> 00:56:43.516
in elaborately wrought
metal introduces problems

00:56:43.516 --> 00:56:46.948
of scale, quite similar to
those Cindy illuminates

00:56:46.948 --> 00:56:51.192
in "The Castle of Otranto," in
comparison to other seashells,

00:56:51.192 --> 00:56:54.254
the nautilus is both
remarkable and valuable,

00:56:54.254 --> 00:56:57.520
because it is giant, right,
it's a giant seashell.

00:56:57.520 --> 00:57:00.716
But, as an object, especially one in the,

00:57:00.716 --> 00:57:04.467
when it's made into the cup,
displaying ferocious monsters,

00:57:04.467 --> 00:57:06.601
it's diminutive, so it's a way of,

00:57:06.601 --> 00:57:07.857
like taking these, like, you know,

00:57:07.857 --> 00:57:09.931
kind of like big
monsters, and putting them

00:57:09.931 --> 00:57:14.014
into a shape that you can
put on a mantle, right.

00:57:14.938 --> 00:57:19.659
And if we think about the
interior of the shell as a kind

00:57:19.659 --> 00:57:24.392
of grotto, akin to the
space under the giant helmet

00:57:24.392 --> 00:57:27.384
in which Theodore is
temporarily imprisoned,

00:57:27.384 --> 00:57:30.998
it's a miniature, it's a miniature grotto.

00:57:30.998 --> 00:57:34.700
In fact, I discovered, looking
at a particular nautilus cup

00:57:34.700 --> 00:57:36.921
that was in The Louvre,
and I was so excited

00:57:36.921 --> 00:57:40.180
when I saw this that I, I
actually cursed out loud

00:57:40.180 --> 00:57:43.475
in the museum, it was very
embarrassing, 'cause it echoes.

00:57:43.475 --> 00:57:45.896
But I saw it, I looked
in this nautilus shell,

00:57:45.896 --> 00:57:50.280
and I discovered small
helms were routinely carved

00:57:50.280 --> 00:57:54.204
in the interior of shells
prepared for nautilus cups,

00:57:54.204 --> 00:57:56.159
so that when you peer into the chamber

00:57:56.159 --> 00:58:00.729
of the mounted shell, what
faces you is a tiny helmet.

00:58:00.729 --> 00:58:03.868
And I could say more about
why the artisan did this,

00:58:03.868 --> 00:58:06.827
but I don't have time right
now, so you can ask me later.

00:58:06.827 --> 00:58:07.704
Another thing I learned

00:58:07.704 --> 00:58:11.123
about while researching
nautilus cups was the phenomenon

00:58:11.123 --> 00:58:13.956
of catastrophic positive buoyancy,

00:58:14.929 --> 00:58:18.301
which is something the chambered
nautilus can experience,

00:58:18.301 --> 00:58:21.469
so that's the phrase that scientists use

00:58:21.469 --> 00:58:24.193
to describe the fact the
chambered nautilus likes to be,

00:58:24.193 --> 00:58:25.671
it's, like kind of a bottom dweller.

00:58:25.671 --> 00:58:27.295
It likes to be deep in the water,

00:58:27.295 --> 00:58:29.512
and it can propel itself up and down,

00:58:29.512 --> 00:58:31.734
as well as around in the water,

00:58:31.734 --> 00:58:34.853
but if it, for some
reason, rises too quickly

00:58:34.853 --> 00:58:38.061
to the surface, it can't correct course

00:58:38.061 --> 00:58:41.869
and propel itself back down,
and it dies on the surface,

00:58:41.869 --> 00:58:45.705
so that's catastrophic positive buoyancy.

00:58:45.705 --> 00:58:49.970
But this strikes me as
also a potential metaphor

00:58:49.970 --> 00:58:52.387
for the feeling I associate
with the spectacle

00:58:52.387 --> 00:58:56.401
of poor Conrad's violent
death by a ridiculous object.

00:58:56.401 --> 00:58:58.585
Catastrophic positive buoyancy suggests,

00:58:58.585 --> 00:59:01.441
to me, a form of hysterical laughter,

00:59:01.441 --> 00:59:04.029
one out of control,
and triggered as easily

00:59:04.029 --> 00:59:06.660
by calamity, disaster, and violence,

00:59:06.660 --> 00:59:09.645
as by a good fortune,
one that is as likely

00:59:09.645 --> 00:59:13.476
to feel painful in its
excess as pleasurable.

00:59:13.476 --> 00:59:16.327
As a description of the
spirit that animates "Otranto,"

00:59:16.327 --> 00:59:18.800
the phrase shows us how Walpole's sense

00:59:18.800 --> 00:59:21.478
of humor is, in many ways, more indebted

00:59:21.478 --> 00:59:24.341
to the excesses and
grotesqueries celebrated

00:59:24.341 --> 00:59:28.154
in Greek satyr plays than to Roman satire.

00:59:28.154 --> 00:59:31.511
In its more exuberant
moments, the gothic pushes us

00:59:31.511 --> 00:59:34.187
to a giddy encounter with
the visceral material

00:59:34.187 --> 00:59:36.639
of life and death in a way that seems,

00:59:36.639 --> 00:59:41.311
by measures of classical
reasons, rather unhinged.

00:59:41.311 --> 00:59:45.530
Ultimately, the funniness
of "The Castle of Otranto"

00:59:45.530 --> 00:59:48.715
turns patriarchal emblems and institutions

00:59:48.715 --> 00:59:51.521
into a suicidal killing joke.

00:59:51.521 --> 00:59:56.033
Here, I'm still following
Cindy's lead, she writes,

00:59:56.033 --> 00:59:58.642
"In 'Otranto,' it is almost as if,

00:59:58.642 --> 01:00:02.458
"the bigger the phallus,
the bigger its impotence."

01:00:02.458 --> 01:00:05.036
Which I think is true,
and this also leads me

01:00:05.036 --> 01:00:07.412
to think back to Sandra's description

01:00:07.412 --> 01:00:10.833
of the book as a "folly of ejaculation,"

01:00:10.833 --> 01:00:14.534
maybe in a slightly different sense.

01:00:14.534 --> 01:00:17.468
The stark and inflated hilarity

01:00:17.468 --> 01:00:21.234
of phallic power rendered
impotent is central,

01:00:21.234 --> 01:00:24.107
I would say, to the funniness
of Walpole's writing,

01:00:24.107 --> 01:00:27.242
and it places him in a
genealogy of both intentionally,

01:00:27.242 --> 01:00:30.497
and unintentionally funny English letters,

01:00:30.497 --> 01:00:32.348
extending, at least in my project,

01:00:32.348 --> 01:00:35.357
from Eliza Haywood to Thomas de Quincey.

01:00:35.357 --> 01:00:39.147
But the failure implied
in scenes of impotence,

01:00:39.147 --> 01:00:41.571
right, a failure to consolidate,

01:00:41.571 --> 01:00:44.736
to embody, and to wield power,

01:00:44.736 --> 01:00:47.538
is transformed by their funniness,

01:00:47.538 --> 01:00:51.474
in scenes like these, into
a different kind of agency,

01:00:51.474 --> 01:00:54.120
or, I would like to argue, a political,

01:00:54.120 --> 01:00:56.455
a form of political promise.

01:00:56.455 --> 01:00:58.203
Cindy concludes, in her reading,

01:00:58.203 --> 01:01:02.982
that "'Otranto's' giant things
are meant to be a bit silly."

01:01:02.982 --> 01:01:06.115
Pairing this observation with
Lauren Berlant's insistence

01:01:06.115 --> 01:01:09.074
on the counter politics
of the silly object,

01:01:09.074 --> 01:01:11.495
I've been trying to think
about both the cultural

01:01:11.495 --> 01:01:15.342
and political stakes of
refusing to take the giants

01:01:15.342 --> 01:01:18.811
of one's dominant social order seriously.

01:01:18.811 --> 01:01:22.367
I'm thinking about how "Otranto"
reveals the clumsiness,

01:01:22.367 --> 01:01:25.139
again, the clumsy, this
book is a clumsy thing,

01:01:25.139 --> 01:01:29.297
the clumsiness and agitation
of patriarchal agents,

01:01:29.297 --> 01:01:31.845
the scrambling, ineffectual male heroes,

01:01:31.845 --> 01:01:33.857
but also the absurd helmet kind

01:01:33.857 --> 01:01:37.228
of like tromping around the house,

01:01:37.228 --> 01:01:40.888
of how it dares us, of
how the novel dares us not

01:01:40.888 --> 01:01:43.857
to smirk at the gory revelation

01:01:43.857 --> 01:01:47.503
of their embodied frailty,
those patriarchal agents,

01:01:47.503 --> 01:01:49.796
and of the spectacular vulnerability

01:01:49.796 --> 01:01:53.103
of patriarchal lineage on display

01:01:53.103 --> 01:01:56.417
in Conrad's bleeding, mangled remains.

01:01:56.417 --> 01:02:00.584
And of how the novel refuses
to provide the trappings

01:02:01.564 --> 01:02:06.123
of patriarchal power and
inheritance with a dignified home,

01:02:06.123 --> 01:02:09.557
offering only the
structurally unstable edifice

01:02:09.557 --> 01:02:12.542
of a flagrantly fictional
paper castle that,

01:02:12.542 --> 01:02:16.387
as Cindy points out, crumbles
at the slightest touch.

01:02:16.387 --> 01:02:18.858
As a hero, Theodore is laughable,

01:02:18.858 --> 01:02:21.719
the formal comedy that
should be consolidated

01:02:21.719 --> 01:02:25.210
in his marriage to Isabella is transformed

01:02:25.210 --> 01:02:27.797
by his feebleness as a vision of man

01:02:27.797 --> 01:02:30.424
into a more satirical comedy,

01:02:30.424 --> 01:02:33.223
centered on the catastrophic weakness

01:02:33.223 --> 01:02:36.144
of patriarchy's privileged sons.

01:02:36.144 --> 01:02:38.663
This is the catastrophic positive buoyancy

01:02:38.663 --> 01:02:42.656
that interests me in "Otranto,"
opposite the nautilus,

01:02:42.656 --> 01:02:46.449
whose positive buoyancy
carries it to catastrophe,

01:02:46.449 --> 01:02:49.884
"Otranto's" catastrophes offer to lift us

01:02:49.884 --> 01:02:52.537
by way of their hilarity to a place

01:02:52.537 --> 01:02:55.020
where the death of particular institutions

01:02:55.020 --> 01:02:59.008
of power takes on a festive
energy, and we are invited

01:02:59.008 --> 01:03:02.358
to revel in the ruins of
their dignity, thank you.

01:03:02.358 --> 01:03:05.108
(group applauds)

01:03:10.031 --> 01:03:11.965
- I thank all the
speakers for the richness

01:03:11.965 --> 01:03:15.295
of their papers, and the
brevity of their remarks,

01:03:15.295 --> 01:03:17.861
which brings us out right
to pretty much on time

01:03:17.861 --> 01:03:21.987
for a full discussion with
your questions leading us.

01:03:21.987 --> 01:03:25.402
I will ask you to give
me a chance just to pause

01:03:25.402 --> 01:03:28.491
after you ask your question
so that I can paraphrase it,

01:03:28.491 --> 01:03:31.561
so that your question will be
picked up on the microphone,

01:03:31.561 --> 01:03:35.644
and make the record coherent
when it is reviewed.

01:03:37.379 --> 01:03:38.796
I open the floor.

01:03:40.036 --> 01:03:40.869
- I would actually like

01:03:40.869 --> 01:03:45.594
to see Sean diagram the
nautilus grotto. (laughs)

01:03:45.594 --> 01:03:48.677
- I would like that, too, let's talk.

01:03:52.691 --> 01:03:53.524
- John.

01:03:55.295 --> 01:03:59.462
- (indistinct) Sean, something else 
made me think that this notion of the papier-mache,

01:04:00.521 --> 01:04:03.297
that, where it's covered with stucco,

01:04:03.297 --> 01:04:08.253
and here's this part of the river,
Chiswick and Strawberry Hill, Pope's villa

01:04:08.253 --> 01:04:12.886
close together, on the
tradition of the folly,

01:04:12.886 --> 01:04:14.136
or the playful.

01:04:15.252 --> 01:04:18.814
(background noise drowns out speaker) garden building

01:04:18.814 --> 01:04:20.429
Which virtually always is fake,

01:04:20.429 --> 01:04:23.966
it's not made of real stone, you know,

01:04:23.966 --> 01:04:27.308
only when you get to the
rococo, with (indistinct).

01:04:27.308 --> 01:04:31.475
you get a building that asserts its statements

01:04:33.705 --> 01:04:37.872
And so, you know, I was
thinking about the papier-mache,

01:04:39.788 --> 01:04:43.955
and the idea of Strawberry
Hill's being, I hope,

01:04:44.962 --> 01:04:46.129
on your terms,

01:04:47.735 --> 01:04:50.152
a kind of network description

01:04:52.182 --> 01:04:55.555
of the gothic, not, not the gothic itself.

01:04:55.555 --> 01:04:59.679
(background noise drowns out speaker)

01:04:59.679 --> 01:05:03.762
Series of inscribed
statements, and condensations

01:05:05.074 --> 01:05:07.895
about what the gothic is, a
little bit like his own book,

01:05:07.895 --> 01:05:10.787
the, the book on English painting and art
1270
01:05:10,787 --&gt; 01:05:13,655
(background noise drowns out speaker)

01:05:13.655 --> 01:05:16.721
Published before
"Otranto's", I just am trying

01:05:16.721 --> 01:05:18.917
to put something out for people to,

01:05:18.917 --> 01:05:21.781
to think about, that on Strawberry Hill,

01:05:21.781 --> 01:05:23.676
I'm sure you all have been there,

01:05:23.676 --> 01:05:27.093
I mean, it's small, it's manifestly fake,

01:05:29.284 --> 01:05:30.577
I haven't seen the restoration,

01:05:30.577 --> 01:05:34.330
but I assume that underlines the fakeness,

01:05:34.330 --> 01:05:36.611
and in the dream, Cindy, one wonders

01:05:36.611 --> 01:05:40.556
how the giant made it up
that papier-mache staircase.

01:05:40.556 --> 01:05:41.929
(group laughs)

01:05:41.929 --> 01:05:45.502
Which is so small, and
so fragile that the,

01:05:45.502 --> 01:05:46.485
so I'm just trying to.

01:05:46.485 --> 01:05:48.968
(background noise drowns out speaker)

01:05:48.968 --> 01:05:51.563
- John Bender's question
is about the artifice

01:05:51.563 --> 01:05:55.700
of the folly, and of the
papier-mache world created

01:05:55.700 --> 01:06:00.233
around such a folly, and even
including the language is,

01:06:00.233 --> 01:06:04.447
if I heard you right, of, the
descriptive language creating

01:06:04.447 --> 01:06:07.364
that fairy tale castle in the mind.

01:06:08.903 --> 01:06:11.886
I, was that directed particularly at Sean,

01:06:11.886 --> 01:06:12.820
but anyone, yeah.

01:06:12.820 --> 01:06:17.336
- What I heard in it,
that you were asking Sean,

01:06:17.336 --> 01:06:21.402
was when you, when you called
what he was doing a kind

01:06:21.402 --> 01:06:24.747
of description of the gothic,
rather than the gothic,

01:06:24.747 --> 01:06:27.747
itself, right, your, your images as,

01:06:29.888 --> 01:06:33.027
I guess in my term, an
ekphrasis of the gothic,

01:06:33.027 --> 01:06:37.616
rather than, right, and so
that's what I would like

01:06:37.616 --> 01:06:40.976
to hear you talk about,
because what I was struck by is

01:06:40.976 --> 01:06:44.858
that each of you, of the rest of you,

01:06:44.858 --> 01:06:48.025
were doing something that I didn't do,

01:06:49.627 --> 01:06:53.203
which was to set up a kind of isomorphism

01:06:53.203 --> 01:06:57.370
between these formal patterns
that you were registering,

01:07:00.102 --> 01:07:02.626
although Jesse, that might
not be true, but like,

01:07:02.626 --> 01:07:05.709
for you, Sean, there's an isomorphism

01:07:06.848 --> 01:07:11.015
between this computer formalization
of cognitive processes,

01:07:12.070 --> 01:07:16.237
right, and these architectural
forms, and then that leads

01:07:17.240 --> 01:07:21.407
to a kind of claim, or a,
the stakes of that involve,

01:07:22.918 --> 01:07:27.085
sort of, re-imagining the
moment of the 18th century

01:07:28.166 --> 01:07:31.511
as a connectionist, rather
than a reductionist,

01:07:31.511 --> 01:07:33.591
so that's functionalist, right.

01:07:33.591 --> 01:07:36.570
And then in, you know,
for Cindy, there was

01:07:36.570 --> 01:07:40.133
an isomorphism established
between these syntactic patterns

01:07:40.133 --> 01:07:42.599
that you were noticing, and,

01:07:42.599 --> 01:07:46.165
and I guess I ultimately
wrote down "genre," right,

01:07:46.165 --> 01:07:48.047
so a certain sort of--
- And architectural.

01:07:48.047 --> 01:07:51.296
- Yeah, as well as (indistinct), 
and then, you know, for Eugenia,

01:07:51.296 --> 01:07:52.793
there was this isomorphism,

01:07:52.793 --> 01:07:56.548
and perhaps yours was
the strongest argument

01:07:56.548 --> 01:08:01.322
that moved from this formal
isomorphism to a claim about,

01:08:01.322 --> 01:08:05.467
in this case, politics, so
I was really struck by that,

01:08:05.467 --> 01:08:09.907
that sort of, the
repetition of that structure

01:08:09.907 --> 01:08:13.174
to the way we make arguments,
and so I just wanted

01:08:13.174 --> 01:08:14.739
to sort of throw that out as

01:08:14.739 --> 01:08:18.446
what I picked out from John's question.

01:08:18.446 --> 01:08:21.029
A model of the sort they make in 
architectural school

01:08:23.755 --> 01:08:25.772
Rather than a building.

01:08:25.772 --> 01:08:28.341
- I mean, I, I guess what I would,

01:08:28.341 --> 01:08:32.127
I guess the question bears
on how deep we're willing

01:08:32.127 --> 01:08:35.377
to allow the aesthetic to run, if it's,

01:08:36.550 --> 01:08:38.786
I mean, the notion of, so I guess I would,

01:08:38.786 --> 01:08:40.507
I would object to the, not object,

01:08:40.507 --> 01:08:42.215
that's too strong, but I would object,

01:08:42.215 --> 01:08:43.493
in a soft sense, I suppose, to the.

01:08:43.493 --> 01:08:44.326
(group laughs)

01:08:44.326 --> 01:08:45.716
To the, to the, to the notion of,

01:08:45.716 --> 01:08:48.443
to the notion of
Strawberry Hill as a model.

01:08:48.443 --> 01:08:50.490
I would unstrenuously
object to the notion of,

01:08:50.490 --> 01:08:52.952
or strenuously, no, and instead, I mean,

01:08:52.952 --> 01:08:55.669
I think of it, really, as
a residue to a process.

01:08:55.669 --> 01:08:57.108
You know, one of the things we forget,

01:08:57.108 --> 01:08:58.503
and I think, categorically forget

01:08:58.503 --> 01:09:00.804
about Strawberry Hill is how
many times it was remodeled

01:09:00.804 --> 01:09:03.127
and rebuilt, and it was often remodeled

01:09:03.127 --> 01:09:06.071
and rebuilt not simply by Walpole himself,

01:09:06.071 --> 01:09:08.028
but in consultation with other people,

01:09:08.028 --> 01:09:09.733
and also with other people in mind,

01:09:09.733 --> 01:09:11.232
like people he wanted to visit,

01:09:11.232 --> 01:09:13.113
or people that he wanted
to have live there,

01:09:13.113 --> 01:09:16.377
often other men, but not always other men.

01:09:16.377 --> 01:09:18.837
And what's left now is,
of course, an object

01:09:18.837 --> 01:09:21.601
that we tour, and it stands
as a kind of ornament book

01:09:21.601 --> 01:09:24.610
of gothic style, but it
wasn't built that way,

01:09:24.610 --> 01:09:26.331
right, it was built over time.

01:09:26.331 --> 01:09:28.658
Some of the things I'm saying
here are relatively obvious,

01:09:28.658 --> 01:09:31.166
but when I say that I
would experience it more

01:09:31.166 --> 01:09:33.780
as a residue than as
a model, is that it's,

01:09:33.780 --> 01:09:35.081
it's clearly a residue of a process

01:09:35.081 --> 01:09:38.253
that unfolded over years,
over about 30 years,

01:09:38.253 --> 01:09:41.716
that were also Walpole's most
productive literary years,

01:09:41.716 --> 01:09:44.564
but not as a model in the
sense that it's something

01:09:44.564 --> 01:09:47.779
that was ever held in
the mind whole, and then,

01:09:47.779 --> 01:09:49.263
you know, and then projected in the way

01:09:49.263 --> 01:09:51.544
that sometimes we think
of models as conceptual,

01:09:51.544 --> 01:09:53.825
and also as material.

01:09:53.825 --> 01:09:57.501
- Can I ask why you say "residue
of a process," rather than?

01:09:57.501 --> 01:09:58.334
- Yeah.

01:09:58.334 --> 01:10:00.764
- Well, I think a lot of the
joy of Strawberry Hill was,

01:10:00.764 --> 01:10:03.516
you know, changing it, working with it,

01:10:03.516 --> 01:10:06.216
you know, continually building,
that's why the papier-mache,

01:10:06.216 --> 01:10:08.444
or the wallpaper, so, I
mean, if it's in stone,

01:10:08.444 --> 01:10:09.958
it's in stone, but if it's wallpaper,

01:10:09.958 --> 01:10:13.291
you could throw it up,
and see how it looks and,

01:10:13.291 --> 01:10:16.019
unchanged, but, then, of
course, it's left over, right?

01:10:16.019 --> 01:10:19.169
The thing is left there after
the remodeling has been done.

01:10:19.169 --> 01:10:21.268
- I mean, he said that because he wants

01:10:21.268 --> 01:10:24.488
for it not to be an object, right, Sean?

01:10:24.488 --> 01:10:25.393
- Me?
- Yeah.

01:10:25.393 --> 01:10:26.510
- Yeah, well, that's--

01:10:26.510 --> 01:10:29.389
- Because to be an object is to be reduced

01:10:29.389 --> 01:10:32.794
in some way, and you
want it to be an index

01:10:32.794 --> 01:10:35.371
of a process, rather than--

01:10:35.371 --> 01:10:36.724
- He wanted to have positive buoyancy--

01:10:36.724 --> 01:10:38.757
- Which is why I think there's
an interesting connection

01:10:38.757 --> 01:10:42.590
between the way you're
sort of producing this,

01:10:43.984 --> 01:10:46.737
this sign of genre as
it's happening, right,

01:10:46.737 --> 01:10:48.421
as it's sort of unfolding, Cindy,

01:10:48.421 --> 01:10:50.801
and what, in this sort of, I think,

01:10:50.801 --> 01:10:55.145
conceptual, or theoretical
investments of your argument,

01:10:55.145 --> 01:10:59.312
Sean, in process, rather
than, say, objecthood or objectness.

01:11:00.565 --> 01:11:03.011
- I just wanted to add to that,

01:11:03.011 --> 01:11:05.750
to go back to John's
observation about the,

01:11:05.750 --> 01:11:07.077
the ghost, right, like the giant,

01:11:07.077 --> 01:11:09.414
the image of the giant ghost moving

01:11:09.414 --> 01:11:12.831
through this very small, you know, house.

01:11:14.050 --> 01:11:17.025
I think that, that the
way that bodies move

01:11:17.025 --> 01:11:22.013
through the actual house
of Strawberry Hill is also

01:11:22.013 --> 01:11:24.868
something that the
space is doing, I don't,

01:11:24.868 --> 01:11:26.914
I don't know that it was
designed, exactly, right,

01:11:26.914 --> 01:11:29.206
it wasn't like, modeled so that, you know,

01:11:29.206 --> 01:11:31.275
bodies would move in
a particular way, but.

01:11:31.275 --> 01:11:32.683
And this is something
that I had never thought

01:11:32.683 --> 01:11:35.466
about until I actually
found myself in the space,

01:11:35.466 --> 01:11:37.879
'cause you kind of have to be in it

01:11:37.879 --> 01:11:41.310
to realize the proportions of it,

01:11:41.310 --> 01:11:44.177
like the proportions are
off from what you're led

01:11:44.177 --> 01:11:48.361
to imagine you would
feel from images of it,

01:11:48.361 --> 01:11:50.538
and I think that's part
of what it's doing,

01:11:50.538 --> 01:11:53.960
but this was, this was
brought home pretty starkly,

01:11:53.960 --> 01:11:58.560
the first time that I went,
so this is like in fall, 2014,

01:11:58.560 --> 01:12:00.662
and it was the first time that I'd gone

01:12:00.662 --> 01:12:04.175
after the restoration, and the docent

01:12:04.175 --> 01:12:06.187
that was kind of showing me around,

01:12:06.187 --> 01:12:09.127
originally, took me up the staircase,

01:12:09.127 --> 01:12:11.131
and on the banisters, they had these,

01:12:11.131 --> 01:12:14.027
you know, beautiful little deer ornaments

01:12:14.027 --> 01:12:17.618
that, it refers to the family
seal, or, you know, something,

01:12:17.618 --> 01:12:21.920
and on the second floor, one
of them was missing, and, like,

01:12:21.920 --> 01:12:25.058
it, like it looked like
it had just been torn off.

01:12:25.058 --> 01:12:27.996
And the docent apologized
that it was such a mess,

01:12:27.996 --> 01:12:30.027
and they hadn't, you
know, like cleaned it up,

01:12:30.027 --> 01:12:31.626
or, like, you know, repaired it.

01:12:31.626 --> 01:12:35.140
But he said, just the day or two before,

01:12:35.140 --> 01:12:37.396
someone had come in with a giant backpack,

01:12:37.396 --> 01:12:38.796
and as they rounded the corner,

01:12:38.796 --> 01:12:41.819
they just knocked the deer
right off the banister.

01:12:41.819 --> 01:12:43.311
But that's kind of what it's like

01:12:43.311 --> 01:12:45.009
to move around in that space,

01:12:45.009 --> 01:12:47.500
so just being like a
normal-sized human being,

01:12:47.500 --> 01:12:50.629
or maybe like a slightly
larger, you know, than normal,

01:12:50.629 --> 01:12:53.796
you feel, you feel the odd proportions

01:12:55.133 --> 01:12:59.163
of your body in relation
to the space around you.

01:12:59.163 --> 01:13:01.278
- Can I say one more
thing about the artifice?

01:13:01.278 --> 01:13:02.769
And that is that, I mean, it's important

01:13:02.769 --> 01:13:04.903
for us to acknowledge that, I mean,

01:13:04.903 --> 01:13:06.837
the very term "gothic," I mean,

01:13:06.837 --> 01:13:09.169
it's referring explicitly to a typeface,

01:13:09.169 --> 01:13:11.646
I mean, it's referring explicitly
to its status as print.

01:13:11.646 --> 01:13:13.337
And Cindy, actually, when you were,

01:13:13.337 --> 01:13:15.250
you know, giving your presentation,

01:13:15.250 --> 01:13:18.036
one of the most absurd lines to me is,

01:13:18.036 --> 01:13:21.740
when Theodore, we have,
"He printed 1,000 kisses

01:13:21.740 --> 01:13:25.060
"on her clay cold hands,
and uttered every expression

01:13:25.060 --> 01:13:27.038
"that despairing love could dictate."

01:13:27.038 --> 01:13:29.874
I asked my undergraduates,
can you imagine printing,

01:13:29.874 --> 01:13:31.698
you know, that many number of kisses?

01:13:31.698 --> 01:13:33.151
And, you know, part of, I think,

01:13:33.151 --> 01:13:35.866
that, the absurdity comes
through the recognition

01:13:35.866 --> 01:13:38.381
that we're dealing with printed words,

01:13:38.381 --> 01:13:41.115
you know, here on a page, and so,

01:13:41.115 --> 01:13:42.770
you know, I think there's
a strong attention

01:13:42.770 --> 01:13:46.579
to drawing our attention
to the, the status

01:13:46.579 --> 01:13:48.773
of objects as codes for something.

01:13:48.773 --> 01:13:50.686
And, you know, the clock
is a code for something,

01:13:50.686 --> 01:13:53.833
it signals time, but the
conception of time is really kind

01:13:53.833 --> 01:13:55.907
of matter, you know,
modeled on the movement

01:13:55.907 --> 01:13:57.648
of the clock, circularly, rather than

01:13:57.648 --> 01:13:59.948
what it actually implies.

01:13:59.948 --> 01:14:02.267
(background noise drowns out speaker)

01:14:02.267 --> 01:14:04.641
- Architectural models as working objects,

01:14:04.641 --> 01:14:08.095
but there's attention in
Walpole's own behavior between

01:14:08.095 --> 01:14:10.757
that fragility, on one hand, and his wish

01:14:10.757 --> 01:14:15.418
to render the building permanent
in the book he published,

01:14:15.418 --> 01:14:19.096
showing these rooms, you
know, so there's a, there's a,

01:14:19.096 --> 01:14:22.845
and that book projects
them, to go to your point.

01:14:22.845 --> 01:14:24.461
The rooms don't really look like that,

01:14:24.461 --> 01:14:27.801
the rooms in the engravings
look much more monumental

01:14:27.801 --> 01:14:31.861
and significant than they
look in the actual building.

01:14:31.861 --> 01:14:34.482
- The extra illustrations
in that book play into

01:14:34.482 --> 01:14:38.232
that, as well, process,
not residue, exactly,

01:14:39.303 --> 01:14:43.408
but ongoing, interpenetrative,
and upsetting,

01:14:43.408 --> 01:14:47.575
or readjusting the perspectives
with every addition.

01:14:49.104 --> 01:14:50.172
- Ruth?

01:14:50.172 --> 01:14:51.722
- This is not so much a
question, as a comment,

01:14:51.722 --> 01:14:53.468
but I was struck, Eugenia,
in your description

01:14:53.468 --> 01:14:55.322
of moving around the building,

01:14:55.322 --> 01:14:57.863
and the knocked-off deer thing,

01:14:57.863 --> 01:15:00.711
it seems like, it read like a reversal

01:15:00.711 --> 01:15:03.327
of what the novel does, right,

01:15:03.327 --> 01:15:06.928
in which making these huge objects,

01:15:06.928 --> 01:15:09.406
knocking down, destroying
the fragile people,

01:15:09.406 --> 01:15:11.819
and, you know, I wonder
if that isn't the kind of,

01:15:11.819 --> 01:15:15.525
you know, the part of the
gap between the small,

01:15:15.525 --> 01:15:18.281
the small actual staircase,
and the (indistinct)

01:15:18.281 --> 01:15:19.829
new staircases, and doing that kind

01:15:19.829 --> 01:15:21.691
of flipping around, I mean--
- Yeah.

01:15:21.691 --> 01:15:24.827
- But it seems so, you
know, it seems so close to.

01:15:24.827 --> 01:15:26.550
(background noise drowns out speaker)

01:15:26.550 --> 01:15:27.978
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:15:27.978 --> 01:15:30.672
- Professor Yeazell has
inverted the relationship

01:15:30.672 --> 01:15:32.477
of backpack to helmet.

01:15:32.477 --> 01:15:35.399
(group laughs)

01:15:35.399 --> 01:15:38.153
(overlapping chatter)

01:15:38.153 --> 01:15:39.153
- Brilliant.

01:15:42.368 --> 01:15:44.260
- Well, I wanted just
to follow up on this,

01:15:44.260 --> 01:15:47.515
on this wonderful discussion, in part by,

01:15:47.515 --> 01:15:49.525
I think, maybe, continuing along

01:15:49.525 --> 01:15:52.324
what Sandra introduced, of how

01:15:52.324 --> 01:15:54.590
(background noise drowns out speaker) one's encountered

01:15:54.590 --> 01:15:56.822
The gothic structure
provides a kind of allegory

01:15:56.822 --> 01:15:58.954
for the way that one reads the novel,

01:15:58.954 --> 01:16:01.428
and, and maybe to see if we can just bring

01:16:01.428 --> 01:16:04.678
that out a bit, and I want for us to,

01:16:05.615 --> 01:16:09.852
maybe for us to think, alongside
the Strawberry Hill as a model,

01:16:09.852 --> 01:16:12.763
there's, of course, "The
Castle of Otranto" itself

01:16:12.763 --> 01:16:16.288
as a model, and there we actually,

01:16:16.288 --> 01:16:18.557
as Sandra pointed out, we
don't really quite know

01:16:18.557 --> 01:16:21.516
what it looks like, but
one thing that we do know,

01:16:21.516 --> 01:16:24.004
and this maybe, this is a
question for the whole panel,

01:16:24.004 --> 01:16:26.163
but I'll begin with Sean, in this respect,

01:16:26.163 --> 01:16:29.117
is that we do know that
it's kind of (indistinct).

01:16:29.117 --> 01:16:32.709
In a sense that the helmet is not buoyant,

01:16:32.709 --> 01:16:35.540
in fact, it cracks a
hole, and that hole allows

01:16:35.540 --> 01:16:38.484
for the moonlight to go
and shine on the people,

01:16:38.484 --> 01:16:40.990
and then to illuminate
the passage that's below.

01:16:40.990 --> 01:16:43.682
So it's like surface, and
then there's an interior,

01:16:43.682 --> 01:16:48.059
and below the interior there's a passage,
all of which provide an account

01:16:48.059 --> 01:16:50.924
of death, you know, whether or not

01:16:50.924 --> 01:16:53.121
that's the functional
and computational model

01:16:53.121 --> 01:16:56.430
that you offered, it might be one

01:16:56.430 --> 01:16:58.367
that's completely (indistinct),
which seems like a kind

01:16:58.367 --> 01:17:01.550
of model of the monument of unconscious,

01:17:01.550 --> 01:17:04.633
but in any case, it's a, a, it's not,

01:17:05.597 --> 01:17:08.666
it doesn't seem to me like a flat neural net,

01:17:08.666 --> 01:17:11.416
connectionist, you know, emergent process

01:17:11.416 --> 01:17:13.475
that your were describing,
but it's one that's sort

01:17:13.475 --> 01:17:16.848
of really much more
hermeneutic in its structure.

01:17:16.848 --> 01:17:18.149
So that, using that term,

01:17:18.149 --> 01:17:21.423
I think I'm suggesting the
(indistinct) of allegories

01:17:21.423 --> 01:17:24.815
for the way in which we read
it, (indistinct), you know,

01:17:24.815 --> 01:17:26.403
descriptive paraphrase
on the model of like

01:17:26.403 --> 01:17:27.945
what Sandra was saying,
or whether or not we're

01:17:27.945 --> 01:17:32.758
actually doing something like
interpretation. (indistinct)

01:17:32.758 --> 01:17:33.591
- Yeah, no, I--

01:17:33.591 --> 01:17:35.049
(overlapping chatter)

01:17:35.049 --> 01:17:37.163
- Jonathan Kramnick has asked a question

01:17:37.163 --> 01:17:41.038
about the nature of the
castle, Strawberry Hill,

01:17:41.038 --> 01:17:45.205
as an architectural edifice,
and the castle in the novel,

01:17:46.072 --> 01:17:49.362
as an allegory of reading for the text.

01:17:49.362 --> 01:17:51.061
- Yeah, no, that's good,
Jonathan, thanks for that.

01:17:51.061 --> 01:17:53.943
I mean, I'll say two things,
one's mildly technical,

01:17:53.943 --> 01:17:55.464
as I understand it, which is that,

01:17:55.464 --> 01:17:58.197
I mean, this is an old objection
to the neural net model,

01:17:58.197 --> 01:18:00.269
is that it would presumably be incapable

01:18:00.269 --> 01:18:03.990
of having higher order
representations of itself,

01:18:03.990 --> 01:18:06.069
and this is Jerry
Fodor's objection to the.

01:18:06.069 --> 01:18:07.159
But it, but that's, I mean,

01:18:07.159 --> 01:18:09.274
that's actually been
empirically disproven,

01:18:09.274 --> 01:18:11.437
that there have been neural networks

01:18:11.437 --> 01:18:13.693
that return descriptions of themselves.

01:18:13.693 --> 01:18:16.692
So that's a, in the
sense that that kind of,

01:18:16.692 --> 01:18:18.431
like higher order, lower
order, the sort of,

01:18:18.431 --> 01:18:20.074
that structural distinction that we seem

01:18:20.074 --> 01:18:23.495
to detect in human
cognition between higher

01:18:23.495 --> 01:18:25.817
and lower order processes,
that that can be simulated

01:18:25.817 --> 01:18:27.138
by a neural network in the same way

01:18:27.138 --> 01:18:29.246
that a neural network could be simulated

01:18:29.246 --> 01:18:31.863
by a functionalist system, but the.

01:18:31.863 --> 01:18:33.096
Yeah, the other thing
that I wanted to say,

01:18:33.096 --> 01:18:35.336
this really, I guess, goes
back to my earlier work

01:18:35.336 --> 01:18:38.438
on thinking about Walpole, thinking

01:18:38.438 --> 01:18:41.537
about "The Castle of Otranto,"
and especially about,

01:18:41.537 --> 01:18:43.747
you know, his other
great gothic experiment,

01:18:43.747 --> 01:18:45.960
"The Mysterious Mother," is the extent

01:18:45.960 --> 01:18:50.575
to which, the illusions of
interiority that emerge,

01:18:50.575 --> 01:18:52.447
or the effects of interiority, I'm talking

01:18:52.447 --> 01:18:55.305
about human psychic interiority,

01:18:55.305 --> 01:18:59.615
emerge in a complex, cognitive ecology of,

01:18:59.615 --> 01:19:02.053
kind of a dialogue, or
an elaborated dialogue

01:19:02.053 --> 01:19:04.536
of relationship between
persons and spaces.

01:19:04.536 --> 01:19:05.892
Right, so that, that's, I mean,

01:19:05.892 --> 01:19:06.950
that would be, I guess, in a way my,

01:19:06.950 --> 01:19:08.711
what do we call it, fallback position?

01:19:08.711 --> 01:19:10.271
Or where I draw my line in the sand is,

01:19:10.271 --> 01:19:13.969
is that there's, Walpole
doesn't seem to me,

01:19:13.969 --> 01:19:16.250
at least, to be sketching out a model,

01:19:16.250 --> 01:19:17.388
which I, in some ways actually argue

01:19:17.388 --> 01:19:19.407
for in the last, in the last book,

01:19:19.407 --> 01:19:21.909
and which I'm now
starting to argue against,

01:19:21.909 --> 01:19:25.352
of a strict kind of a,
what we might almost call

01:19:25.352 --> 01:19:29.164
a quasi-dualist model of
the, you know, of conscious,

01:19:29.164 --> 01:19:32.073
and then, you know,
subconscious interiority,

01:19:32.073 --> 01:19:35.786
but instead elaborates that in these, in,

01:19:35.786 --> 01:19:39.751
in these ecological relationships
with person and place.

01:19:39.751 --> 01:19:42.628
- I'd like to elaborate on that, too,

01:19:42.628 --> 01:19:46.985
on Jonathan's point about
depth being interpretive,

01:19:46.985 --> 01:19:49.497
not just psychological, but generic,

01:19:49.497 --> 01:19:51.959
and part of the reading process.

01:19:51.959 --> 01:19:55.366
That passage, where I pointed out

01:19:55.366 --> 01:19:58.906
that Manfred leaves, and comes back,

01:19:58.906 --> 01:20:02.104
and we have that little textual space

01:20:02.104 --> 01:20:04.748
of other time, other place, it's something

01:20:04.748 --> 01:20:07.644
that the gothic develops especially well,

01:20:07.644 --> 01:20:10.727
kind of pulling apart time and space.

01:20:11.964 --> 01:20:15.464
Think of "The Monk," when Ambrosio sinks on,

01:20:16.762 --> 01:20:19.341
into Matilda's arms, "I am thine,"

01:20:19.341 --> 01:20:23.552
and 15 minutes later, and
130 pages later, (laughs)

01:20:23.552 --> 01:20:28.474
he pops back up in a different
volume, and it's over.

01:20:28.474 --> 01:20:32.641
And there are so many
textual, subterranean passages

01:20:33.479 --> 01:20:36.600
that way that, you know,
other novels make use of,

01:20:36.600 --> 01:20:39.173
too, but I really think
that's one of the kind

01:20:39.173 --> 01:20:42.756
of architectural
innovations of the gothic.

01:20:43.872 --> 01:20:46.083
- I'd like to build on that, and I think,

01:20:46.083 --> 01:20:49.603
possibly, also respond
to Ruth's observation

01:20:49.603 --> 01:20:51.980
about the, the physical vulnerability

01:20:51.980 --> 01:20:55.541
of the built environment,
in relation to the,

01:20:55.541 --> 01:20:59.208
the bodies that move, the human
bodies that move through it.

01:20:59.208 --> 01:21:01.714
But I think that, I
think one of the things

01:21:01.714 --> 01:21:03.754
that's funny, to me, at least,

01:21:03.754 --> 01:21:07.190
about the gothic ruins, like the castle,

01:21:07.190 --> 01:21:09.596
in "The Castle of Otranto," has to do

01:21:09.596 --> 01:21:13.235
with the way that the
novel is playing with time,

01:21:13.235 --> 01:21:17.945
and in another way, and the
relationship between time

01:21:17.945 --> 01:21:22.176
and ruination, like historical
processes of ruination,

01:21:22.176 --> 01:21:24.524
because one of the things that the gothic,

01:21:24.524 --> 01:21:26.338
this gothic story is doing,

01:21:26.338 --> 01:21:29.398
is it ruins things instantaneously, right?

01:21:29.398 --> 01:21:32.585
So it's like you think of
the gothic ruin as something

01:21:32.585 --> 01:21:37.406
that has been ruined over
some extended period of time,

01:21:37.406 --> 01:21:41.435
and therefore, you know,
speaks to historical time,

01:21:41.435 --> 01:21:43.971
in a particular way is, like evidence

01:21:43.971 --> 01:21:48.371
of the way material
doesn't last, you know,

01:21:48.371 --> 01:21:51.723
when you think about time
in a big picture way.

01:21:51.723 --> 01:21:55.565
But, but what's happening
to, just, for example,

01:21:55.565 --> 01:21:59.178
like the castle here, is
it's just being destroyed,

01:21:59.178 --> 01:22:03.582
right before our eyes, it's
being ruined immediately.

01:22:03.582 --> 01:22:08.188
And so, I think that, I think
that that's something that,

01:22:08.188 --> 01:22:10.848
I think this was kind of
like an ongoing project

01:22:10.848 --> 01:22:13.029
of Walpole's, in a certain way,

01:22:13.029 --> 01:22:16.153
I think he was interested
in what happens when you,

01:22:16.153 --> 01:22:19.226
like, what you make
when you ruin something.

01:22:19.226 --> 01:22:20.954
And so that's, that's one of the things

01:22:20.954 --> 01:22:23.285
that I think the castle is, he's doing

01:22:23.285 --> 01:22:26.096
with the castle in the story.

01:22:26.096 --> 01:22:28.322
The frontispiece is a (indistinct) portrait.

01:22:28.322 --> 01:22:30.082
Yeah, that's, yeah, is there,

01:22:30.082 --> 01:22:34.249
I'm seeing if I've got a
picture of that in my edition.

01:22:35.618 --> 01:22:36.451
- Yes?

01:22:36.451 --> 01:22:39.007
- I have a question for Sandra.

01:22:39.007 --> 01:22:40.647
I love the idea that the novel

01:22:40.647 --> 01:22:45.455
is thin and flat (indistinct)

01:22:45.455 --> 01:22:46.288
And I gather--

01:22:46.288 --> 01:22:48.097
- Thin and dead, I think.

01:22:48.097 --> 01:22:49.538
- Well, I gather from your reading

01:22:49.538 --> 01:22:52.332
that one of the things that (indistinct),
would involve is

01:22:52.332 --> 01:22:55.047
this kind of anthropomorphism,
or, I'm sorry,

01:22:55.047 --> 01:22:56.413
isomorphism that--
- Right.

01:22:56.413 --> 01:22:58.237
- That we're talking about here.

01:22:58.237 --> 01:23:00.585
- I mean, I said I
ventriloquized a couple.

01:23:00.585 --> 01:23:01.418
- Yeah.

01:23:01.418 --> 01:23:02.564
- Ways you could do that, but I--

01:23:02.564 --> 01:23:04.750
- In more, even coming
out, right, an allegory--

01:23:04.750 --> 01:23:07.716
- Yes, exactly, yeah, yeah.

01:23:07.716 --> 01:23:10.670
- So I guess I just
want to hear you respond

01:23:10.670 --> 01:23:13.621
to these kind of
isomorphisms, what's at stake

01:23:13.621 --> 01:23:16.212
in resistance in your resistance, but also,

01:23:16.212 --> 01:23:18.310
what's at stake in the
way the novel is seeming

01:23:18.310 --> 01:23:19.905
to invite them in--

01:23:19.905 --> 01:23:22.618
- Oh, I mean, I, I'm totally persuaded

01:23:22.618 --> 01:23:27.195
by each of these absolutely
virtuosic allegorical readings

01:23:27.195 --> 01:23:30.916
of form, totally, I love
it, I do, I mean, come on.

01:23:30.916 --> 01:23:34.107
I do that, we all do that when we teach,

01:23:34.107 --> 01:23:37.207
you know, I just did it
with my undergraduates,

01:23:37.207 --> 01:23:40.280
you know, we talked about interruption,

01:23:40.280 --> 01:23:43.260
this inability to follow whose speaking,

01:23:43.260 --> 01:23:46.580
right, as a sort of, you know, objective,

01:23:46.580 --> 01:23:49.781
correlative for the
thematics of usurpation,

01:23:49.781 --> 01:23:53.251
et cetera, et cetera, I could do it,

01:23:53.251 --> 01:23:57.418
and I, and I think, you know,
and I'm powerfully persuaded

01:23:58.359 --> 01:24:02.949
by these sorts of
arguments, I just was trying

01:24:02.949 --> 01:24:05.468
to think about what it would look like

01:24:05.468 --> 01:24:09.886
to resist doing it, whether
we can resist doing it,

01:24:09.886 --> 01:24:14.448
either just in a, like
in a sort of banal way.

01:24:14.448 --> 01:24:17.222
You know, whether reading
is always, I mean,

01:24:17.222 --> 01:24:19.626
in a sense, it is always comprehension,

01:24:19.626 --> 01:24:22.663
you know, we're not reading
just morphemes, right?

01:24:22.663 --> 01:24:25.182
We're always reading
at the syntactic level,

01:24:25.182 --> 01:24:28.432
and therefore, at the level of meaning,

01:24:30.061 --> 01:24:32.894
and so, you know, so there's that,

01:24:33.987 --> 01:24:37.237
and then there's also just this sort of

01:24:39.068 --> 01:24:40.068
disciplinary

01:24:41.743 --> 01:24:45.076
force of that move from form to meaning.

01:24:47.007 --> 01:24:49.680
And so, really, I'm just
being, I don't know,

01:24:49.680 --> 01:24:53.111
perverse is maybe, I just,
I'm just really curious,

01:24:53.111 --> 01:24:56.361
as a kind of intellectual, theoretical,

01:24:58.179 --> 01:25:02.346
you know, endeavor, game, I
don't, just, what it, what,

01:25:03.960 --> 01:25:07.956
you know, what it, what would
happen if you didn't do that?

01:25:07.956 --> 01:25:11.350
And then maybe all that
happens is a kind of lame,

01:25:11.350 --> 01:25:14.206
like deixis, right, where,
you know, it's like,

01:25:14.206 --> 01:25:17.568
look, look at all these
exclamation points, cool.

01:25:17.568 --> 01:25:19.834
(group laughs)

01:25:19.834 --> 01:25:20.667
You know?

01:25:22.719 --> 01:25:25.142
- One alternative, or
something that might happen,

01:25:25.142 --> 01:25:28.695
like the desire for
description that Heather Love

01:25:28.695 --> 01:25:33.090
provides an account of therefore you
don't interpret as described.

01:25:33.090 --> 01:25:35.064
- Well, yeah, but she doesn't do that.

01:25:35.064 --> 01:25:37.872
She doesn't do it. Right, right, right.

01:25:37.872 --> 01:25:40.627
- Could it be that this fulfills
the Lovean remit, just saying that

01:25:40.627 --> 01:25:43.364
exclamation points, check it out.

01:25:43.364 --> 01:25:45.314
- But I'm not sure I even did,

01:25:45.314 --> 01:25:47.731
I'm not sure I even did that,

01:25:48.606 --> 01:25:50.796
because it's embarrass, I mean.

01:25:50.796 --> 01:25:53.018
(group laughs)

01:25:53.018 --> 01:25:56.424
It's so unscholarly to do that.

01:25:56.424 --> 01:25:59.494
- Well, I guess I wanted
a little more elaboration,

01:25:59.494 --> 01:26:01.162
'cause I don't quite get it,

01:26:01.162 --> 01:26:04.078
I guess I feel fundamentally
aesthetic design,

01:26:04.078 --> 01:26:06.324
down to the ground, but--

01:26:06.324 --> 01:26:08.679
- Oh, that, I would say that this,

01:26:08.679 --> 01:26:13.650
that if we say that The
Castle of Otranto is a game

01:26:13.650 --> 01:26:17.733
in which the entire 100
pages is a literalization

01:26:18.647 --> 01:26:22.019
of the phrase, "grown
too large to inhabit it,"

01:26:22.019 --> 01:26:24.718
I'm not saying it's not designed.

01:26:24.718 --> 01:26:27.036
- I guess what I, (indistinct)
was what's the difference

01:26:27.036 --> 01:26:29.595
in the, in the kind of suggested reading,

01:26:29.595 --> 01:26:31.149
or non-interpretive description

01:26:31.149 --> 01:26:34.649
that's pre-critical, versus post-critical?

01:26:36.208 --> 01:26:38.732
- Um, well, you know,
the post-critique stuff

01:26:38.732 --> 01:26:42.481
often reproduces the descriptive turns,

01:26:42.481 --> 01:26:45.702
turn to eros, or ethos, as, you know,

01:26:45.702 --> 01:26:49.104
the alternative to
hermeneutics, or something.

01:26:49.104 --> 01:26:51.287
Or, it's, sometimes the form it takes is

01:26:51.287 --> 01:26:55.370
an interest in phenomenology
of reading, or, and,

01:26:56.891 --> 01:26:58.974
so what that says, to me,

01:27:00.038 --> 01:27:03.121
is that whatever is being longed for,

01:27:04.742 --> 01:27:08.409
or described in these
theoretical positions,

01:27:10.071 --> 01:27:12.155
it's not actually being fulfilled,

01:27:12.155 --> 01:27:14.952
that the desire is not actually a desire

01:27:14.952 --> 01:27:19.119
for literality, because there
has to be something more

01:27:19.955 --> 01:27:23.049
at stake in what we do than that,

01:27:23.049 --> 01:27:27.082
and it has to be called
"ethics," or "politics,"

01:27:27.082 --> 01:27:30.175
or the eros of art, or whatever, right?

01:27:30.175 --> 01:27:33.325
So, I think I'm mostly just interested

01:27:33.325 --> 01:27:36.175
in the theoretical project, and kind

01:27:36.175 --> 01:27:38.592
of like pushing back against,

01:27:40.481 --> 01:27:44.064
not against the drive
for pure description,

01:27:45.509 --> 01:27:48.176
but against the idea that, that,

01:27:49.925 --> 01:27:54.353
that that's actually what
people who are calling

01:27:54.353 --> 01:27:58.247
for the turn are
doing, and then maybe,

01:27:58.247 --> 01:28:00.726
maybe then to say, maybe
they're not doing it

01:28:00.726 --> 01:28:04.094
because we can't do it, but
I think they're not doing it

01:28:04.094 --> 01:28:08.052
because in Heather Love's
case, she cannot abide the idea

01:28:08.052 --> 01:28:12.538
of not giving a political
account of "Beloved,"

01:28:12.538 --> 01:28:16.866
that is intolerable to us,
c.f. my forthcoming essay

01:28:16.866 --> 01:28:18.333
called "The Political Fallacy."

01:28:18.333 --> 01:28:19.843
- Wait, can I ask a followup question,

01:28:19.843 --> 01:28:23.063
though, of Sandra, is
that alright, about this?

01:28:23.063 --> 01:28:26.880
I wonder if, like this is
such an earnest question,

01:28:26.880 --> 01:28:29.380
I want to know what you think.

01:28:30.234 --> 01:28:33.817
Is Walpole's "Description
of the Villa" doing

01:28:35.029 --> 01:28:39.196
to his house what an actual
descriptive reading would do

01:28:40.949 --> 01:28:43.873
to the text, would do to the novel?

01:28:43.873 --> 01:28:46.892
That is, like the "Description
of the Villa" is this,

01:28:46.892 --> 01:28:50.733
I mean, I find it this
incredibly puzzling text.

01:28:50.733 --> 01:28:53.228
I've tried really hard to use it,

01:28:53.228 --> 01:28:56.894
you know, to make use of it, but it's,

01:28:56.894 --> 01:28:58.879
but it's just a catalog,
I mean, that's all,

01:28:58.879 --> 01:29:00.700
that's what it is, it's just a catalog,

01:29:00.700 --> 01:29:03.532
and, so there's a part of
me that's like, well I,

01:29:03.532 --> 01:29:04.924
you know, I'm good at reading,

01:29:04.924 --> 01:29:07.588
I'm good at interpreting,
I can interpret a catalog.

01:29:07.588 --> 01:29:09.912
There's actually something about it

01:29:09.912 --> 01:29:12.487
that stymies interpretation, you know,

01:29:12.487 --> 01:29:15.179
you kind of find yourself, you're just,

01:29:15.179 --> 01:29:17.315
you're just reading a stark list,

01:29:17.315 --> 01:29:19.318
with very little, every once in awhile,

01:29:19.318 --> 01:29:22.162
there's a little bit of
contextual information,

01:29:22.162 --> 01:29:26.299
but very little, and so if you
read it from cover to cover,

01:29:26.299 --> 01:29:30.078
what you've done is just
taken this descriptive view,

01:29:30.078 --> 01:29:32.661
room by room, of all the spaces

01:29:33.639 --> 01:29:38.401
in a way that doesn't leave
you with anything, really.

01:29:38.401 --> 01:29:40.085
I mean, I guess you have some knowledge

01:29:40.085 --> 01:29:42.157
of what was there, you know, what kind

01:29:42.157 --> 01:29:45.763
of objects were there,
but, is that the kind of--

01:29:45.763 --> 01:29:48.690
- I mean, I'm really interested in that,

01:29:48.690 --> 01:29:51.190
that form, actually, and so I,

01:29:52.365 --> 01:29:56.222
so this week, we read
"Roderick Random," and I'm, I,

01:29:56.222 --> 01:29:59.774
in doing research on the
novel, I came across this essay

01:29:59.774 --> 01:30:03.274
from, like 1979 by a linguist, who's like,

01:30:04.647 --> 01:30:08.509
"Literary critics don't have
any respect for us linguists,

01:30:08.509 --> 01:30:12.353
"us, like serious, scientific,
structural linguists.

01:30:12.353 --> 01:30:16.181
"And so everyone talks
about Smollett's style,

01:30:16.181 --> 01:30:19.270
"but nobody ever says
how his style works."

01:30:19.270 --> 01:30:22.072
So this guy goes ahead and gives this,

01:30:22.072 --> 01:30:24.787
you know, morphology,
linguistic morphology

01:30:24.787 --> 01:30:28.140
of the structure of Smollett's sentences,

01:30:28.140 --> 01:30:29.631
and then goes on to show,

01:30:29.631 --> 01:30:32.074
like how this structure is repeated,

01:30:32.074 --> 01:30:35.003
and it's, so it's like,
you know, I don't know,

01:30:35.003 --> 01:30:37.317
subordinate clause, plus verb, you know,

01:30:37.317 --> 01:30:40.067
I mean, I, I can't do it, myself.

01:30:41.218 --> 01:30:43.885
But I, so I was like, "So, kids,

01:30:45.517 --> 01:30:50.155
"folks, what do we, you
know, what do we, like,"

01:30:50.155 --> 01:30:52.804
they're supposed to write,
like ekphrases this week

01:30:52.804 --> 01:30:55.031
of something, they can either write,

01:30:55.031 --> 01:30:58.352
you know, doing, a verbal
ekphrasis of an object,

01:30:58.352 --> 01:31:00.827
they can do, you know,
they can, I don't care,

01:31:00.827 --> 01:31:03.577
like 3D print an object that's like,

01:31:03.577 --> 01:31:06.444
you know, a reading of
"The Castle of Otranto."

01:31:06.444 --> 01:31:09.152
So I was like, you know, what, is this

01:31:09.152 --> 01:31:10.584
what we should be doing, is this

01:31:10.584 --> 01:31:13.256
what the fulfillment
of Heather Love's call

01:31:13.256 --> 01:31:16.087
for description would look like?

01:31:16.087 --> 01:31:18.382
And we sort of decided, well, A,

01:31:18.382 --> 01:31:21.402
we don't know how to do
that, we're not trained

01:31:21.402 --> 01:31:22.319
to do that,

01:31:23.997 --> 01:31:27.947
but, you know, I forget what B was, but.

01:31:27.947 --> 01:31:31.022
(group laughs)

01:31:31.022 --> 01:31:32.542
(overlapping chatter)

01:31:32.542 --> 01:31:34.514
But yeah, I'm interested in what happened

01:31:34.514 --> 01:31:37.467
to morphological sort of thinking

01:31:37.467 --> 01:31:40.050
after structuralism, and, you know,

01:31:40.050 --> 01:31:44.582
it seems to me like this
return to formalism,

01:31:44.582 --> 01:31:48.981
it, it is not, weirdly, a
return to structuralism,

01:31:48.981 --> 01:31:52.564
or even some aspects
of post-structuralism,

01:31:53.720 --> 01:31:57.442
whereas you would think
that a true commitment

01:31:57.442 --> 01:32:00.023
to pure description would look a lot more

01:32:00.023 --> 01:32:03.642
like structural linguistics,
than like anything else,

01:32:03.642 --> 01:32:07.172
but we don't want that, I
don't think we want that.

01:32:07.172 --> 01:32:09.543
I mean, I might, but I don't think,

01:32:09.543 --> 01:32:12.229
you know, Heather Love does.

01:32:12.229 --> 01:32:13.221
- Yes?

01:32:13.221 --> 01:32:15.640
- I just had a brief
followup to that (indistinct),

01:32:15.640 --> 01:32:17.547
I really enjoyed your description

01:32:17.547 --> 01:32:20.380
of Bianca's failed simile, so the,

01:32:21.288 --> 01:32:23.966
that's kind of the (indistinct),
because it strikes me

01:32:23.966 --> 01:32:25.380
that another way of thinking about this,

01:32:25.380 --> 01:32:27.642
like failure of interpretation,

01:32:27.642 --> 01:32:29.997
or this (indistinct)
interpretation the sublime,

01:32:29.997 --> 01:32:33.642
so like, you, Bianca,
like looks at the helmet,

01:32:33.642 --> 01:32:35.851
and can't describe it,
because that's part of,

01:32:35.851 --> 01:32:36.893
that's part of like the failure,

01:32:36.893 --> 01:32:39.689
the sublime being a kind of
failure of representation.

01:32:39.689 --> 01:32:43.434
And so then, the failure,
that kind of funniness,

01:32:43.434 --> 01:32:47.211
potentially, of "Castle of Otranto" being 
part of the failure of the sublime.

01:32:47.211 --> 01:32:48.551
So I was just wondering if there's a way

01:32:48.551 --> 01:32:50.318
to kind of link those two.

01:32:50.318 --> 01:32:52.953
- Well, but Bianca can't, it's not

01:32:52.953 --> 01:32:56.414
that Bianca can't describe it, she,

01:32:56.414 --> 01:33:00.413
I mean, you could say she
can't, she also won't.

01:33:00.413 --> 01:33:03.429
I mean, it comes at the
end of that whole encounter

01:33:03.429 --> 01:33:07.596
with Manfred, where she's
continually using language

01:33:08.641 --> 01:33:12.808
in a way to, you know, so she's
continually picking up sort

01:33:13.647 --> 01:33:16.897
of literal syntax as a way of resisting

01:33:17.916 --> 01:33:20.677
what Manfred wants to know, which is,

01:33:20.677 --> 01:33:22.613
where is Isabella, or, you know, what,

01:33:22.613 --> 01:33:25.996
what does Isabella feel about
Theodore or whatever, right?

01:33:25.996 --> 01:33:30.214
So, it's a strategy, or at
least it comes at the end

01:33:30.214 --> 01:33:33.899
of a passage, or an
exchange, that's very much

01:33:33.899 --> 01:33:37.066
about a strategic use of indexicality,

01:33:37.922 --> 01:33:41.089
or a sort of refusal of, you know, to,

01:33:43.146 --> 01:33:45.813
to represent, that is not a kind

01:33:46.658 --> 01:33:49.311
of ontological failure,

01:33:49.311 --> 01:33:53.104
epistemological failure, but a strategy.

01:33:53.104 --> 01:33:55.327
So that's my (indistinct), and it's funny,

01:33:55.327 --> 01:33:57.239
you know, it's part of
the wit of that moment,

01:33:57.239 --> 01:34:01.035
is Bianca is this sort
of wily servant figure,

01:34:01.035 --> 01:34:05.478
her, you know, her, I don't
know, I don't know what it,

01:34:05.478 --> 01:34:08.817
like indirection of speech, right?

01:34:08.817 --> 01:34:10.424
- Yeah, yeah.

01:34:10.424 --> 01:34:11.257
- Yes?

01:34:12.154 --> 01:34:15.581
- I have what I think
is a followup question.

01:34:15.581 --> 01:34:18.009
When you're talking, you
made me think of this,

01:34:18.009 --> 01:34:19.701
this is kind of as an aside,

01:34:19.701 --> 01:34:21.573
Adorno has this line
somewhere where he says,

01:34:21.573 --> 01:34:23.083
"The literal is the barbaric,"

01:34:23.083 --> 01:34:24.218
and you were making me think, the literal,

01:34:24.218 --> 01:34:27.659
which may be the gothic, and, you know,

01:34:27.659 --> 01:34:31.495
that's a roundabout way of
asking about the relationship

01:34:31.495 --> 01:34:33.676
between aesthetics and cognition,

01:34:33.676 --> 01:34:35.811
'cause that seems like it's
in the background here.

01:34:35.811 --> 01:34:38.258
(background noise drowns out speaker)

01:34:38.258 --> 01:34:40.136
Fairly descriptive
literal reading would in,

01:34:40.136 --> 01:34:42.303
in some ways be to engage in a kind

01:34:42.303 --> 01:34:45.293
of non-cognitive aesthetics, or something,

01:34:45.293 --> 01:34:47.411
or at least the fear of that?

01:34:47.411 --> 01:34:48.311
(background noise drowns out speaker)

01:34:48.311 --> 01:34:52.464
Interpretation is the
kind of, like buzzword

01:34:52.464 --> 01:34:57.458
that becomes a site of
contestation between these two,

01:34:57.458 --> 01:35:01.062
modes of reading it, I guess, this
is kind of long-winded,

01:35:01.062 --> 01:35:04.125
and maybe trailing off prematurely,

01:35:04.125 --> 01:35:05.988
but I wondered if, like there's a,

01:35:05.988 --> 01:35:08.884
an option besides the two we kind of,

01:35:08.884 --> 01:35:10.518
he kind of sketched in response to.

01:35:10.518 --> 01:35:12.057
(background noise drowns out speaker)

01:35:12.057 --> 01:35:16.545
Interpretation, or a
resistance of interpretation,

01:35:16.545 --> 01:35:18.432
maybe there's something like, you know,

01:35:18.432 --> 01:35:23.367
interpretation whose goal
is to get beyond itself,

01:35:23.367 --> 01:35:24.450
you know, interpretation might,

01:35:24.450 --> 01:35:27.429
like exhaust its
possibilities through sort

01:35:27.429 --> 01:35:31.945
of like similes that don't simulate, 
that don't get it right.

01:35:31.945 --> 01:35:34.174
I don't know how this works,
particularly in relation

01:35:34.174 --> 01:35:36.726
to "The Castle of Otranto,"
but I think it's an argument

01:35:36.726 --> 01:35:40.810
that Paul Fry says
arrives with romanticism,

01:35:40.810 --> 01:35:44.977
but maybe, on your reading,
it arrives with the gothic.

01:35:46.355 --> 01:35:47.188
Yeah.

01:35:47.188 --> 01:35:48.729
- Part of this question
is about the relation

01:35:48.729 --> 01:35:52.562
of aesthetics and cognition
in interpretation.

01:35:54.513 --> 01:35:58.680
- I mean, Sean, what do you
think of this idea that,

01:35:59.624 --> 01:36:00.791
that a literal

01:36:02.805 --> 01:36:05.739
criticism raises the specter

01:36:05.739 --> 01:36:08.156
of a non-cognitive aesthetic?

01:36:08.993 --> 01:36:11.598
That's what I heard you say, initially.

01:36:11.598 --> 01:36:14.844
- Yes, it's an interesting question, and.

01:36:14.844 --> 01:36:17.019
In a sense it cuts to
the core of what it is

01:36:17.019 --> 01:36:19.830
that we think we do when we're thinking,

01:36:19.830 --> 01:36:21.008
whether we think that we're fundamentally

01:36:21.008 --> 01:36:25.362
about association, or
whether we think we're

01:36:25.362 --> 01:36:29.336
about storage and
processing, I'm thinking,

01:36:29.336 --> 01:36:33.196
I'm still thinking about
Walpole's relationship

01:36:33.196 --> 01:36:36.938
to description, and, as Gena pointed out,

01:36:36.938 --> 01:36:40.413
the, his most descriptive text, his,

01:36:40.413 --> 01:36:42.986
the "Description
of the Villa," which was remodeled,

01:36:42.986 --> 01:36:46.609
you know, published twice,
and it's different in each.

01:36:46.609 --> 01:36:49.625
But that it's, it's sustaining,

01:36:49.625 --> 01:36:52.137
my sense is that it works, if you read it,

01:36:52.137 --> 01:36:54.309
and you're not satisfied.
- Right, right.

01:36:54.309 --> 01:36:55.358
- And that that's why there's a ticket

01:36:55.358 --> 01:36:57.791
to go visit the house that's included.

01:36:57.791 --> 01:36:58.802
- Yeah.

01:36:58.802 --> 01:37:00.799
- Because that "Description" has a,

01:37:00.799 --> 01:37:02.405
inasmuch as it's a human document,

01:37:02.405 --> 01:37:04.892
I mean, inasmuch as it's
an informational document,

01:37:04.892 --> 01:37:06.815
you know, art historians can use it

01:37:06.815 --> 01:37:10.094
to establish provenance, it could be,

01:37:10.094 --> 01:37:11.880
it's, it is, of Walpole's documents,

01:37:11.880 --> 01:37:13.861
it is the one that could
most easily be converted

01:37:13.861 --> 01:37:15.653
into a database, I think, you know?

01:37:15.653 --> 01:37:16.486
- Yeah.

01:37:16.486 --> 01:37:19.027
- These are the things that he
had, here's where they were.

01:37:19.027 --> 01:37:21.426
But, but it's also the document that most,

01:37:21.426 --> 01:37:25.825
maybe not most, but for
effect, let's say most,

01:37:25.825 --> 01:37:28.127
relies on the space that it imagines,

01:37:28.127 --> 01:37:30.051
in that, that in fact,
it draws you to the space

01:37:30.051 --> 01:37:32.131
that it imagines, which
is to say that it's,

01:37:32.131 --> 01:37:35.795
it's in a, a kind of
intense dialogue with,

01:37:35.795 --> 01:37:38.800
an associative dialogue,
I think, with the,

01:37:38.800 --> 01:37:40.368
with the house that it records.

01:37:40.368 --> 01:37:43.433
So, yeah, I guess would say that--

01:37:43.433 --> 01:37:46.489
- Would you call it poiesis?

01:37:46.489 --> 01:37:47.584
This listing of what's there--

01:37:47.584 --> 01:37:49.151
- I think you might call
it, I, no, I wouldn't.

01:37:49.151 --> 01:37:50.672
(group laughs)

01:37:50.672 --> 01:37:52.997
That's what I would ask you
what you would call it.

01:37:52.997 --> 01:37:54.965
- Could it, I mean, it's non-narrative,

01:37:54.965 --> 01:37:58.129
but it is a narrative of space.

01:37:58.129 --> 01:37:59.731
- And a movement through it.

01:37:59.731 --> 01:38:02.037
- Yeah, I mean, that's,
yeah, just to be clear,

01:38:02.037 --> 01:38:03.547
I mean, that's the other
thing that it's been quite,

01:38:03.547 --> 01:38:06.439
it's been pretty rigorously established

01:38:06.439 --> 01:38:08.251
that it wasn't designed
as an auction catalog

01:38:08.251 --> 01:38:09.674
so much as it was designed as a hand list

01:38:09.674 --> 01:38:12.340
for a tour that you weren't taking yet.

01:38:12.340 --> 01:38:15.451
- But it also sets up, I mean, I think,

01:38:15.451 --> 01:38:18.279
this is kind of a speculative statement,

01:38:18.279 --> 01:38:22.760
but I think it also sets
up a certain encounter

01:38:22.760 --> 01:38:25.984
with the objects, once you
actually take your ticket

01:38:25.984 --> 01:38:30.151
and go there, that itself is
designed to be unsatisfying.

01:38:31.641 --> 01:38:34.399
Like, I think that, I mean, just,

01:38:34.399 --> 01:38:37.252
just kind of based on
my own experience of,

01:38:37.252 --> 01:38:39.692
you, having read this "Description,"

01:38:39.692 --> 01:38:42.341
become very enamored of the idea

01:38:42.341 --> 01:38:46.063
of getting a better
experience with these objects,

01:38:46.063 --> 01:38:48.467
right, like this "Description"
is telling me a little bit,

01:38:48.467 --> 01:38:49.684
but what it's really telling me is

01:38:49.684 --> 01:38:52.232
that I don't really
know what this thing is,

01:38:52.232 --> 01:38:55.463
I would understand it better if I saw it,

01:38:55.463 --> 01:38:58.373
you know, in actual space, ideally,

01:38:58.373 --> 01:39:00.692
if I saw it in Strawberry Hill, right,

01:39:00.692 --> 01:39:02.396
and I know there's been,
like all of these attempts

01:39:02.396 --> 01:39:04.370
to get the objects back into the house,

01:39:04.370 --> 01:39:07.612
so people can have that experience.

01:39:07.612 --> 01:39:10.705
But, but then, you know,
you come to the object,

01:39:10.705 --> 01:39:13.859
and, I don't, I mean, I'm saying
this as a literary scholar

01:39:13.859 --> 01:39:16.310
who's been trying to, like
work with material objects,

01:39:16.310 --> 01:39:18.050
or read material objects,
like you get there,

01:39:18.050 --> 01:39:19.588
and you're in front of
it, and you're like,

01:39:19.588 --> 01:39:22.303
that's cool, it's a little bit
like the exclamation points,

01:39:22.303 --> 01:39:26.345
right, it's like, that's cool,
but I'm not having some kind

01:39:26.345 --> 01:39:29.410
of revelatory experience unlocked simply

01:39:29.410 --> 01:39:31.884
by having come into contact with it,

01:39:31.884 --> 01:39:35.551
so it's, it's like it
begins a narrative of,

01:39:36.884 --> 01:39:41.051
of continual unsatisfying
contact with, with the object.

01:39:42.320 --> 01:39:45.617
- You know, I was just
thinking, as you said this,

01:39:45.617 --> 01:39:49.558
one of the, we read Virginia Woolf in our,

01:39:49.558 --> 01:39:53.141
my graduate class, and
the essays that Mrs.

01:39:54.927 --> 01:39:56.137
- Bennett and, right.

01:39:56.137 --> 01:39:59.331
- Mrs. Brown, where she
talks about the experience

01:39:59.331 --> 01:40:01.492
of reading a lot of 18th century text,

01:40:01.492 --> 01:40:03.394
"Tristram Shandy" and "Pride and Prejudice,"

01:40:03.394 --> 01:40:06.042
as completely satisfying,
when you're done,

01:40:06.042 --> 01:40:07.220
all you want to do is pick it up,

01:40:07.220 --> 01:40:09.243
read it again, understand it better,

01:40:09.243 --> 01:40:12.547
unlike her, the late 19th century,

01:40:12.547 --> 01:40:14.391
and her contemporaries.

01:40:14.391 --> 01:40:15.647
But I wonder, I mean, we've been talking

01:40:15.647 --> 01:40:19.420
about the gothic
architecturally and literarily

01:40:19.420 --> 01:40:23.587
as unsatisfying, with that
fall out of that kind of,

01:40:24.676 --> 01:40:29.163
and does that Woolfian criticism
even play a part in your,

01:40:29.163 --> 01:40:31.779
in sort of finding a middle ground,

01:40:31.779 --> 01:40:34.529
and interpretively, in any sense?

01:40:35.405 --> 01:40:37.430
Does that make any sense?

01:40:37.430 --> 01:40:39.162
- Just very quickly, I think, I mean,

01:40:39.162 --> 01:40:42.564
the other, it's Walpole's
other gothic experiment,

01:40:42.564 --> 01:40:44.222
I think, the, you know,
"The Mysterious Mother,"

01:40:44.222 --> 01:40:47.352
that is his argument
about the relationship

01:40:47.352 --> 01:40:50.602
between desire, and the object, and it,

01:40:51.891 --> 01:40:55.468
as a play that was famously
published, but that,

01:40:55.468 --> 01:40:58.048
famously publicized,
but withheld from view,

01:40:58.048 --> 01:41:00.212
and that the house, again, reenacts that,

01:41:00.212 --> 01:41:02.713
as a play that you can,
you can never quite see,

01:41:02.713 --> 01:41:03.889
it's announced in the catalog,

01:41:03.889 --> 01:41:05.010
but you're not allowed to read it.

01:41:05.010 --> 01:41:06.657
- And the Beauclerk closet, too,

01:41:06.657 --> 01:41:08.772
like that one kind of secretive space.

01:41:08.772 --> 01:41:12.338
And the drawings no longer exist, right,

01:41:12.338 --> 01:41:14.785
I mean, even when you
go visit the house now,

01:41:14.785 --> 01:41:17.132
they'll tell you, "This
is the Beauclerk closet

01:41:17.132 --> 01:41:21.299
"where the drawings of "The
Mysterious Mother" were displayed

01:41:22.201 --> 01:41:24.817
"for Walpole's own private enjoyment,"

01:41:24.817 --> 01:41:27.491
but I think those drawings
are non-existent, right?

01:41:27.491 --> 01:41:28.683
- They're here.
- Oh, no--

01:41:28.683 --> 01:41:29.695
- Six or seven of them, I think.

01:41:29.695 --> 01:41:31.323
- Oh, okay, sorry, I'm completely,

01:41:31.323 --> 01:41:32.773
I'm completely wrong then, but anyway,

01:41:32.773 --> 01:41:34.171
there is this kind of, (laughs)

01:41:34.171 --> 01:41:36.393
this is this setup of revelation--

01:41:36.393 --> 01:41:38.527
- And the manuscript for the play was kept

01:41:38.527 --> 01:41:40.528
in a cabinet there, too.
- Okay.

01:41:40.528 --> 01:41:41.378
- Yeah, but there's the famous,

01:41:41.378 --> 01:41:42.869
there's several encounters of people

01:41:42.869 --> 01:41:44.995
with those drawings, after
having read about them,

01:41:44.995 --> 01:41:48.116
these celebrated drawings,
and there's a marvelous note

01:41:48.116 --> 01:41:50.621
in one of the auction catalogs, when,

01:41:50.621 --> 01:41:54.788
when they were finally seen,
and he just simply says,

01:41:55.711 --> 01:41:57.294
"They're actually rather ordinary,

01:41:57.294 --> 01:42:00.389
"and out of context, there's
nothing to recommend them."

01:42:00.389 --> 01:42:01.639
- Right, right.

01:42:03.415 --> 01:42:04.967
- Yes, Sarah?

01:42:04.967 --> 01:42:07.080
- So I was just thinking
about this question,

01:42:07.080 --> 01:42:09.513
about whether there's a third way

01:42:09.513 --> 01:42:12.810
of reading between description
and interpretation,

01:42:12.810 --> 01:42:15.732
and I was wondering if phenomenology,

01:42:15.732 --> 01:42:19.856
or a phenomenological
account of reading is,

01:42:19.856 --> 01:42:22.884
is not, our answer to
what that third way is,

01:42:22.884 --> 01:42:27.497
because when you, to get, to
read in a phenomenological way,

01:42:27.497 --> 01:42:29.477
as I take it, is to give a report

01:42:29.477 --> 01:42:32.371
of the encounter with the text,

01:42:32.371 --> 01:42:36.206
which is not the same
as giving a description,

01:42:36.206 --> 01:42:38.915
because it's not pretending
that it's not filtered

01:42:38.915 --> 01:42:42.853
through a consciousness, and, you know,

01:42:42.853 --> 01:42:44.258
I was just trying to think about all,

01:42:44.258 --> 01:42:45.532
what is it that I'm trying to do

01:42:45.532 --> 01:42:47.645
when I write about "The Castle of Otranto,"

01:42:47.645 --> 01:42:49.863
or any other text, and I was
thinking, it's something,

01:42:49.863 --> 01:42:53.263
like well, what I think
I'm doing is asking myself

01:42:53.263 --> 01:42:56.038
how it's asking to be interpreted.

01:42:56.038 --> 01:42:57.157
And, of course, you could say,

01:42:57.157 --> 01:43:00.527
well that's, you know,
that's, whatever your answer

01:43:00.527 --> 01:43:03.159
to that question is is
itself an interpretation,

01:43:03.159 --> 01:43:07.511
but I feel like the, the
phenomenological answer would

01:43:07.511 --> 01:43:11.678
be the in telling you how
I think this text is asking

01:43:12.856 --> 01:43:16.827
to be interpreted, that is my account of

01:43:16.827 --> 01:43:20.108
what it is like to encounter
this text, and to say

01:43:20.108 --> 01:43:24.464
that it is offering up these
different possibilities,

01:43:24.464 --> 01:43:28.631
these different ways of
reading it, and the, my job,

01:43:29.572 --> 01:43:33.170
as the critic, is to articulate those,

01:43:33.170 --> 01:43:35.398
and make them explicit in some way.

01:43:35.398 --> 01:43:36.714
So, I don't, I just was wondering

01:43:36.714 --> 01:43:39.015
if phenomenology is a helpful way

01:43:39.015 --> 01:43:42.354
of thinking about a third term,

01:43:42.354 --> 01:43:44.363
in addition to interpretation
and description?

01:43:44.363 --> 01:43:46.243
- Yeah, the question
is about phenomenology

01:43:46.243 --> 01:43:48.600
as an alternative to the descriptive,

01:43:48.600 --> 01:43:50.933
or the interpretive methods.

01:43:52.597 --> 01:43:55.003
- Can I say something,
Sandra, I know this is kind

01:43:55.003 --> 01:43:56.910
of directed to you, but, I mean, for me,

01:43:56.910 --> 01:43:59.367
this is, I mean, it's really kind of one

01:43:59.367 --> 01:44:02.545
of Walpole's most lasting legacies,

01:44:02.545 --> 01:44:05.948
and we get that in the first
preface, is that, you know,

01:44:05.948 --> 01:44:09.542
I wish this novel had more
of a moral than that the sins

01:44:09.542 --> 01:44:13.022
of future generations
would be visited upon you,

01:44:13.022 --> 01:44:16.587
it's kind of a open avowal
of kind of, you know,

01:44:16.587 --> 01:44:20.283
the content-less, or the, you, the,

01:44:20.283 --> 01:44:23.894
our foolishness in
attempting to, you know,

01:44:23.894 --> 01:44:27.232
begin to ask interpretive questions of it,

01:44:27.232 --> 01:44:29.555
I mean, it seems to me that, you know,

01:44:29.555 --> 01:44:32.638
he's, he's very canny, or aware, and,

01:44:33.718 --> 01:44:37.687
you know, seeking to promote
a different kind of reading,

01:44:37.687 --> 01:44:39.881
probably than, you know,
the realist tradition

01:44:39.881 --> 01:44:41.845
of the novel, but, you know, certainly,

01:44:41.845 --> 01:44:43.899
you know, I think it's a, you know,

01:44:43.899 --> 01:44:48.066
it's a, a different way of
looking at art, I think.

01:44:49.923 --> 01:44:53.256
- Going off of that, do you see the sort

01:44:54.122 --> 01:44:57.157
of (indistinct) and intersubjective realism

01:44:57.157 --> 01:45:00.717
of a text like "Mrs.
Dalloway" as a different kind

01:45:00.717 --> 01:45:03.161
of fantasia of the realization?

01:45:03.161 --> 01:45:03.994
- Mm-hmm.

01:45:05.037 --> 01:45:07.690
Yeah, well, I mean, it's an open question,

01:45:07.690 --> 01:45:09.214
I mean, this is certainly, you know,

01:45:09.214 --> 01:45:11.486
one of the, you know, key texts of,

01:45:11.486 --> 01:45:14.205
you know, I would describe
of modernist realism,

01:45:14.205 --> 01:45:16.126
in that, you know, the depiction of the,

01:45:16.126 --> 01:45:19.406
you know, the consciousness, you know,

01:45:19.406 --> 01:45:20.918
such that we have never seen, I think,

01:45:20.918 --> 01:45:25.207
in literature, but, I mean, I
think that it's also important

01:45:25.207 --> 01:45:27.807
to recognize, just to
contradict what I just said,

01:45:27.807 --> 01:45:31.294
that, I mean, there's,
there's extraordinary realism

01:45:31.294 --> 01:45:33.746
in "The Castle of Otranto," in that sort of,

01:45:33.746 --> 01:45:37.200
you know, obsessional looking
for character motivations.

01:45:37.200 --> 01:45:38.910
And, you know, John
Bender, sitting over there,

01:45:38.910 --> 01:45:41.234
is, you know, I think the kind of pioneer

01:45:41.234 --> 01:45:44.565
in recognizing, you know, this work as,

01:45:44.565 --> 01:45:47.493
you know, offering free
and direct discourse,

01:45:47.493 --> 01:45:50.478
really, at a capacity you never see.

01:45:50.478 --> 01:45:52.314
And, you know, if we can describe free

01:45:52.314 --> 01:45:55.801
and direct discourse as
an important feature of,

01:45:55.801 --> 01:45:57.807
you know, modern narrative realism,

01:45:57.807 --> 01:46:00.810
you know, it is there in this
work that declares itself

01:46:00.810 --> 01:46:03.060
to be morally content-less.

01:46:05.821 --> 01:46:08.020
- I mean, I wouldn't, I, myself,

01:46:08.020 --> 01:46:10.389
wouldn't be entirely comfortable

01:46:10.389 --> 01:46:14.534
with the distinction between
realism and the gothic,

01:46:14.534 --> 01:46:16.784
gothicism and realism, but,

01:46:19.571 --> 01:46:21.886
and nor would I associate realism

01:46:21.886 --> 01:46:25.528
with the adumbration of consciousness.

01:46:25.528 --> 01:46:26.361
So,

01:46:27.638 --> 01:46:31.507
I guess I'm sort of, I actually take a lot

01:46:31.507 --> 01:46:36.224
of what I was saying about
literalism as an account

01:46:36.224 --> 01:46:38.224
of its real, of realism,

01:46:39.945 --> 01:46:41.762
and, and for me,

01:46:41.762 --> 01:46:45.941
the, you know, the way
that that literalism,

01:46:45.941 --> 01:46:48.684
for Walpole, is a kind of, is an interest

01:46:48.684 --> 01:46:51.197
in, I guess what I'm trying to,

01:46:51.197 --> 01:46:54.641
sort of thinking of as emblematics,

01:46:54.641 --> 01:46:59.298
sort of, an, you know, an
emblematics of the primary,

01:46:59.298 --> 01:47:03.465
the primary qualities of, or
attributes of certain forms,

01:47:06.489 --> 01:47:09.822
as, you know, sort of how realism works.

01:47:11.832 --> 01:47:13.915
Doors, casements, stairs,

01:47:16.461 --> 01:47:19.242
male bodies, female bodies,

01:47:19.242 --> 01:47:23.409
right, it, it doesn't, when
the human figure emerges,

01:47:24.327 --> 01:47:27.023
it doesn't seem, to me,
substantively different

01:47:27.023 --> 01:47:30.440
from the way doors work in, in the novel.

01:47:31.341 --> 01:47:34.495
So that, you know, that's,
I guess what, and so,

01:47:34.495 --> 01:47:37.817
oh, and as for "Mrs. Dalloway,"
I actually have started

01:47:37.817 --> 01:47:41.484
to think of that novel,
in particular, as a,

01:47:43.048 --> 01:47:47.771
a kind of apotheosis of a
literalism so extreme as

01:47:47.771 --> 01:47:50.677
to be, as a kind of hyper-materiality,

01:47:50.677 --> 01:47:53.584
so that, like Woolf's
aesthetic is so much,

01:47:53.584 --> 01:47:58.033
it's like Mondrian, it's
like colors, shape, line,

01:47:58.033 --> 01:48:00.868
you know, and that's
what interests me more,

01:48:00.868 --> 01:48:05.691
I guess, these days, than
about, about "Mrs. Dalloway,"

01:48:05.691 --> 01:48:07.774
than, than consciousness.

01:48:09.310 --> 01:48:13.942
- We have time for one more
question in this session.

01:48:13.942 --> 01:48:14.775
Yes?

01:48:15.684 --> 01:48:18.724
- I have a question about
funniness as it relates

01:48:18.724 --> 01:48:22.061
to two, I guess sort of adjacent,

01:48:22.061 --> 01:48:23.878
or potentially overlapping categories,

01:48:23.878 --> 01:48:28.045
which would be, I guess the
genre of comedy, and bathos,

01:48:29.371 --> 01:48:33.006
and I was wondering about, just the,

01:48:33.006 --> 01:48:35.211
and I guess, also this connects to,

01:48:35.211 --> 01:48:37.454
to your question about the sort of,

01:48:37.454 --> 01:48:38.973
the potential for inversion
in the relationship

01:48:38.973 --> 01:48:41.194
between the body and the space,

01:48:41.194 --> 01:48:43.944
so that it could be sort of, I don't know,

01:48:43.944 --> 01:48:46.101
a joke on the body, that, as sort

01:48:46.101 --> 01:48:48.687
of perceiving the clumsiness
of one's own body in a space,

01:48:48.687 --> 01:48:51.589
or either the objects or the body start

01:48:51.589 --> 01:48:53.333
to think kind of funny or off.

01:48:53.333 --> 01:48:56.609
And this would, I mean, I, I'm curious

01:48:56.609 --> 01:48:58.811
what you would think about this,
but it seems like one thing

01:48:58.811 --> 01:49:00.665
that distinguishes the
funny would be that kind

01:49:00.665 --> 01:49:03.531
of instability about like,
what, what is the target

01:49:03.531 --> 01:49:06.752
of the joke, or the target of the satire?

01:49:06.752 --> 01:49:09.429
And that made me also
wonder about the status

01:49:09.429 --> 01:49:11.981
of the two prefaces in
relation to one another,

01:49:11.981 --> 01:49:14.596
and that sort of performance of,

01:49:14.596 --> 01:49:16.145
in the editorial history of a kind of joke

01:49:16.145 --> 01:49:18.621
on the reader, and what would that do

01:49:18.621 --> 01:49:21.454
to the sort of, the target of the,

01:49:22.375 --> 01:49:23.639
the sort of imminent target of the,

01:49:23.639 --> 01:49:26.928
of the joke of the book,
which you're suggesting might be sort

01:49:26.928 --> 01:49:28.992
of, like patriarchal,
institutional structure of,

01:49:28.992 --> 01:49:33.087
like, does that sort of,
re-filtering of that adjust

01:49:33.087 --> 01:49:34.869
that meeting in any way, or ask us to?

01:49:34.869 --> 01:49:35.898
- Right, yeah.

01:49:35.898 --> 01:49:38.484
- The question is, if it's
funny, what's the joke?

01:49:38.484 --> 01:49:39.583
(group laughs)

01:49:39.583 --> 01:49:41.897
- Right, yeah, yeah, and that's,

01:49:41.897 --> 01:49:44.229
that's actually a brilliant
way of distilling that,

01:49:44.229 --> 01:49:46.668
'cause I think that what I've,

01:49:46.668 --> 01:49:49.226
what I've come to
understand, more and more,

01:49:49.226 --> 01:49:50.935
as I've been working on this project,

01:49:50.935 --> 01:49:54.935
is that I'm less
interested in the funniness

01:49:54.935 --> 01:49:56.685
of jokes than I am in

01:49:59.333 --> 01:50:00.333
instances of

01:50:02.313 --> 01:50:05.683
funniness arriving from an instability of,

01:50:05.683 --> 01:50:07.814
of maybe we could say,
like relative positions,

01:50:07.814 --> 01:50:09.183
I mean, I'm thinking now about, you know,

01:50:09.183 --> 01:50:11.600
Sean's work on relationships.

01:50:14.203 --> 01:50:16.391
And so, I mean, I'm thinking a little bit

01:50:16.391 --> 01:50:18.042
about your question about the prefaces,

01:50:18.042 --> 01:50:20.136
but it, but it made me
leap to something else,

01:50:20.136 --> 01:50:23.934
which is what I'm going to
actually talk about. (laughs)

01:50:23.934 --> 01:50:26.887
Maybe there's an answer to
your question here somewhere.

01:50:26.887 --> 01:50:30.227
But one of the, like one
of the kinds of funniness

01:50:30.227 --> 01:50:33.998
that I have come to think
about more and more is the way

01:50:33.998 --> 01:50:37.455
that 18th century texts,
like "The Castle of Otranto,"

01:50:37.455 --> 01:50:40.631
are funny to contemporary readers,

01:50:40.631 --> 01:50:43.674
like how they're funny
to us, and funny in a way

01:50:43.674 --> 01:50:46.956
that they're not, that they're
almost certainly not designed

01:50:46.956 --> 01:50:49.829
to be, right, so there's
no reason to think

01:50:49.829 --> 01:50:51.722
that when Walpole was writing this,

01:50:51.722 --> 01:50:54.235
that one of the things he
had anywhere in his mind was,

01:50:54.235 --> 01:50:55.586
what is this, how is this going to read

01:50:55.586 --> 01:50:58.791
to a 21st century reader, (laughs) right?

01:50:58.791 --> 01:51:02.695
But there is, there is a
certain, like very palpable kind

01:51:02.695 --> 01:51:05.658
of funniness to 18th century prose,

01:51:05.658 --> 01:51:07.526
and I think the texture of the prose

01:51:07.526 --> 01:51:10.668
that Cindy was talking
about is a large part of it.

01:51:10.668 --> 01:51:12.952
When students, for example,
like when we teach these texts,

01:51:12.952 --> 01:51:15.140
and students read it for the first time,

01:51:15.140 --> 01:51:17.071
they frequently, like, they'll say, like,

01:51:17.071 --> 01:51:20.668
"I don't get it," or, "I don't
understand what's going on."

01:51:20.668 --> 01:51:25.384
But they're still compelled
through something like curiosity

01:51:25.384 --> 01:51:27.660
that I think that, that's even heightened,

01:51:27.660 --> 01:51:29.339
sometimes, into thinking it's funny,

01:51:29.339 --> 01:51:31.165
what they're looking at is funny.

01:51:31.165 --> 01:51:33.220
And it doesn't mean they
feel like there's a joke

01:51:33.220 --> 01:51:36.763
that they're in on there, it
just means there's something,

01:51:36.763 --> 01:51:38.227
like, I'm trying to put my, I'm trying

01:51:38.227 --> 01:51:39.986
to come up with a way of describing what

01:51:39.986 --> 01:51:42.845
that something is that they're picking up,

01:51:42.845 --> 01:51:45.050
when they say, "Yeah,
this was really funny,"

01:51:45.050 --> 01:51:46.619
I didn't, you know, like what.

01:51:46.619 --> 01:51:48.535
And then the followup
question is, like what,

01:51:48.535 --> 01:51:51.230
what is going on, right, what's happening?

01:51:51.230 --> 01:51:55.138
But I think that that is, is coming out

01:51:55.138 --> 01:51:59.953
of the instability of the
relationship between reader

01:51:59.953 --> 01:52:01.890
and text, and particularly,
like, you know,

01:52:01.890 --> 01:52:04.221
reader, in her own moment in history,

01:52:04.221 --> 01:52:08.388
and text, kind of like speaking
from its moment in history,

01:52:09.694 --> 01:52:12.721
and so, so yeah, like, Walpole,

01:52:12.721 --> 01:52:14.535
so to go back to my point about the way

01:52:14.535 --> 01:52:18.013
that Walpole is playing
with instantaneous ruins,

01:52:18.013 --> 01:52:21.426
I almost feel a little bit
like his multiple prefaces are,

01:52:21.426 --> 01:52:24.479
like speeding up that
process of a text kind

01:52:24.479 --> 01:52:28.302
of revealing itself as a
different text through time,

01:52:28.302 --> 01:52:30.876
through one edition to the next, right?

01:52:30.876 --> 01:52:34.483
It kind of radically reframes the text

01:52:34.483 --> 01:52:38.118
in a way that we might see,
as literary historians,

01:52:38.118 --> 01:52:40.031
or we might interpret as happening,

01:52:40.031 --> 01:52:41.858
like in this kind of organic way,

01:52:41.858 --> 01:52:45.266
just because the world
changes through time,

01:52:45.266 --> 01:52:47.606
and so the things in it change, as well.

01:52:47.606 --> 01:52:49.347
But I think that Walpole
is kind of saying,

01:52:49.347 --> 01:52:52.180
like, no, I mean, maybe, but also,

01:52:53.439 --> 01:52:57.743
we can do this, and
generate those effects kind

01:52:57.743 --> 01:53:00.457
of immediately in the here and now.

01:53:00.457 --> 01:53:02.753
- I'm not going to end
the conversation here,

01:53:02.753 --> 01:53:05.139
but pause it, with the note that one

01:53:05.139 --> 01:53:07.772
of the pleasures, or
among the many pleasures

01:53:07.772 --> 01:53:11.373
of a Country House Weekend,
not shooting birds,

01:53:11.373 --> 01:53:16.058
not chasing foxes, but
carrying on the conversation

01:53:16.058 --> 01:53:19.050
into the next session, so tally ho.

01:53:19.050 --> 01:53:21.800
(group applauds)

